// Auto-generated by scripts/build-aquinas-commentary.cjs (kept fresh by scripts/watch-aquinas-commentary.cjs) — do not edit by hand.
// Paraphrased summaries of St. Thomas Aquinas's Sententia libri Metaphysicae, one per Metaphysics chapter.
// Original wording based on the public-domain Latin text (corpusthomisticum.org) — not a translation of any modern copyrighted edition.
window.AQUINAS_COMMENTARY = {
  "B1C1": "Aquinas reads Aristotle's opening claim - that all men by nature desire to know - as resting on three grounds. First, everything naturally seeks its own perfection, and the intellect's perfection lies precisely in knowing; so the desire to know is simply a thing's desire for its own completion. Second, understanding is the operation proper to man as man, distinguishing him from other creatures, and every nature takes a natural delight in its own proper activity. Third, all intellectual beings have some native longing for union with the higher, separate causes of things, and even our pleasure in mere sensation is a distant sign of that deeper appetite. Aquinas then works through Aristotle's ladder of cognition. Wholly stationary creatures have only the dimmest, most confused awareness. Animals with memory but no hearing, like the bee, display a kind of native skill but cannot be taught, since teaching requires the ability to receive another's words. Animals with memory and hearing together can be taught. Man alone rises further: from repeated memories of the same thing he forms experience, and from experience, by grasping the common cause behind many single cases, he forms art and science. Aquinas stresses that experience knows only that something is so in this or that individual, while art and science know the universal cause and can therefore explain why and can teach. Since wisdom is honored above all other knowledge, and its distinguishing mark is precisely the grasp of causes, wisdom must be understood as the science that knows things through their first and highest causes.",
  "B1C2": "Aquinas draws together the marks people commonly attribute to a wise person - knowing everything in a general way, mastering what is difficult, teaching with precision, seeking knowledge for its own sake, and commanding rather than serving other studies - and argues that all of these converge on one science: the universal study of first causes. Because it treats the most universal principles, such a science implicitly contains knowledge of everything under them; because those principles are furthest from the senses, they are hardest to grasp; because it explains through causes, it alone truly teaches, since teaching is showing why, not merely that; and because it is desired for itself rather than for some further use, it is the freest and most honorable of the sciences, ordering the others as their end. Aquinas then follows Aristotle's account of philosophy's origin in wonder: humanity began by marveling at ordinary puzzles and advanced to greater ones, such as the motions of sun and moon and the origin of the universe, and pursued answers purely to escape ignorance, not for practical gain, which is confirmed by the fact that such inquiry only began once the necessities and comforts of life were already secured. Because this science is sought purely for itself, and because it either belongs to God alone or belongs to him most of all, it is the most divine of the sciences, both as the kind of knowledge most fitting for God to have and as knowledge concerning God as first principle. Aquinas notes that philosophy's proper end reverses its beginning: it starts in wonder at not knowing causes and terminates in the far better state of understanding them, so that wonder is dissolved rather than sustained.",
  "B1C3": "Aquinas takes this chapter as Aristotle's formal introduction of the doctrine of the four causes, already treated in the Physics but now recalled as the framework for reviewing earlier thinkers. The formal cause is a thing's substance or essence, the answer to what it most fundamentally is; the material cause is what it is made from; the efficient cause is the source of change or motion; the final cause is the end or good for the sake of which something is or happens. Aquinas observes that the earliest philosophers, beginning with Thales, grasped only the material cause: they looked for some single underlying stuff - water, air, fire, or several elements together - that persists through all change, reasoning by analogy with how an individual like Socrates remains the same subject despite becoming musical or losing that quality. Because they fixed on this enduring substratum, they denied real generation and corruption of substances, treating all change as mere alteration of one abiding matter. Aquinas points out the deeper inadequacy Aristotle is building toward: a substratum cannot move or change itself, so something beyond matter is needed to explain why change occurs at all - the efficient cause. This is why the chapter ends by introducing Anaxagoras's mind, or reason, as the first tentative appeal to an ordering intelligence behind nature, which Aristotle praises, by comparison with his predecessors, as sounding like a sober man among the reckless. Aquinas treats this moment as the historical hinge where philosophy is pressed by the evidence itself toward recognizing a cause of motion distinct from matter.",
  "B1C4": "Aquinas traces the further pressure that pushed early thinkers toward efficient causality. Once philosophers noticed order and beauty in nature, and not merely brute matter, some, following hints in Hesiod and Parmenides about love as a cosmic force, began to see that a principle uniting things is needed besides the material substratum. Empedocles goes further by positing two contrary efficient principles, friendship and strife, making friendship the cause of the good and orderly and strife the cause of corruption and disorder - and Aquinas credits him as the first, however dimly, to treat good and evil themselves as principles, since whatever causes good things is itself in some sense the good. Yet Aquinas follows Aristotle in judging that both Anaxagoras and Empedocles used their causes only fitfully rather than systematically: Anaxagoras invokes mind only when stuck, otherwise crediting chance causes, and Empedocles is inconsistent about whether love unites or separates things and whether strife does the reverse - like untrained fighters who land good blows without any real command of their art. Aquinas then turns to the atomists, Leucippus and Democritus, who reduced everything to the full and the void, treating fullness as being and void as non-being, and differentiated the atoms only by shape, arrangement, and position, comparable to how letters differ in form, order, and orientation. He notes that, like their predecessors, the atomists never seriously investigated the source of motion itself, leaving the question of what actually causes change unresolved even after multiplying material principles.",
  "B1C5": "Aquinas explains that the Pythagoreans, trained in mathematics, mistook the principles of number - the odd and the even, corresponding to limit and the unlimited - for the principles of all reality, since they observed many likenesses between numerical ratios and things such as justice, soul, and the harmonies of music. Because number seemed to underlie everything, they treated numbers not as abstractions but as the very substance of things, and some among them arranged ten pairs of contraries, including limit and unlimited, odd and even, and good and bad, as the ultimate principles, even inventing a tenth heavenly body, the counter-earth, simply to preserve the perfection of the number ten. Aquinas treats this as an attempt to explain the world through formal, mathematical structure rather than matter, but faults it for confusing what is true only by abstraction and analogy with what is true of independently existing substances. He then turns to those who held that all reality is simply one being: Parmenides, Melissus, and Xenophanes. Aquinas distinguishes them carefully - Parmenides considers the one according to its intelligible nature or definition and so calls it finite, Melissus considers it according to extension in matter and so calls it infinite, while Xenophanes, the vaguest of the three, merely identified the one with God without clarifying which kind of unity he meant. Because a wholly immobile, undifferentiated one cannot generate anything, Aquinas judges that these thinkers, by denying motion altogether, actually abandoned natural philosophy, though Parmenides comes nearer the truth by also positing two subordinate principles, hot and cold, to account for the plurality evident to the senses.",
  "B1C6": "Aquinas presents Plato as shaped early by the Heraclitean teaching, absorbed through Cratylus, that all sensible things are in constant flux and therefore cannot be objects of stable knowledge. From Socrates, who sought fixed universal definitions in ethical matters, Plato drew the further conclusion that if any true, stable definition is possible, its object cannot be the ever-changing things of sense; there must be separately existing realities corresponding to our universal concepts, which he called the Ideas or Forms, and sensible things are named after these and exist by imitating or participating in them. Between the Forms and sensible things Plato also placed the objects of mathematics, eternal and unchanging like the Forms but, unlike them, existing in many likenesses rather than uniquely. Aquinas explains Plato's account of the Forms' own principles: the Great-and-Small serves as their material element, a kind of indeterminate duality, while the One serves as their formal or essential principle, and Numbers arise as the Great-and-Small participates in the One. Aquinas notes that, on this analysis, Plato in the end recognizes only two of the four causes, the formal and the material - the Forms as the essence-cause of sensible things and the One as the essence-cause of the Forms themselves - while assigning the origin of good and evil to these same two elements, much as Empedocles and Anaxagoras had done. He also flags, as the root of Plato's error, the assumption that because the mind can consider a nature apart from its individuating material conditions, that nature must therefore also exist apart from matter in reality.",
  "B1C7": "Aquinas treats this short chapter as Aristotle's summary judgment on the whole historical survey just completed. Reviewing every thinker considered, he finds that none named any cause outside the fourfold division already established, yet none named any cause with real clarity either. The material cause was recognized by nearly everyone, whether they supposed one principle or several, corporeal or otherwise. The efficient cause, the source of motion, was grasped only by a few, such as those who invoked love and strife or an ordering mind. The formal cause - the essence of things - was touched only obscurely by those committed to the Forms, since they made the Forms the essence of sensible things and the One the essence of the Forms, without ever properly distinguishing this from the other causes. The final cause, the end for the sake of which things exist and change, was acknowledged only incidentally: thinkers who called the One or being itself good treated goodness as somehow bound up with the first principle, but never actually claimed that things exist or come to be for the sake of that good, so that they spoke of the good as a cause without really meaning it as a cause in the proper sense. Aquinas draws the conclusion that this collective, if fragmentary, testimony of the ancients itself confirms the completeness and correctness of Aristotle's own division of causes into exactly these four, since no one, straining in every direction, ever discovered a fifth kind.",
  "B1C8": "Aquinas now follows Aristotle's critical turn against the philosophers just surveyed. Against those who posited a single corporeal principle, he gives three charges: they ignore incorporeal realities, such as the soul, by admitting only bodily principles; they cannot consistently account for motion even though their own project of explaining generation and corruption requires it; and they never posit a formal cause, so they cannot say what a thing truly is, only what it is made from. He then shows that their own criteria for ranking the elements pull in opposite directions: judged by simplicity, the finest and most rarefied element, fire, should be first; judged by the order of generation, the densest and most compounded, earth, should be first, since composition follows simplicity in time. Aquinas resolves the tension by distinguishing what is prior in a particular process of coming-to-be, where potency precedes act in time, from what is prior absolutely, where act and perfection must come first - suggesting that only an incorporeal first principle could combine maximal simplicity with maximal perfection, which bodily elements cannot. Turning to Empedocles, Aquinas notes the added difficulties of elements generating one another despite being called first, of positing two independent contrary movers rather than one prior source, and of placing contraries like hot and cold in wholly separate subjects, which makes real alteration between them impossible. He treats Anaxagoras more sympathetically: read charitably, the claim that all things were originally mixed except mind gestures toward matter as pure indeterminate potency and mind as pure separate actuality, though taken literally it yields absurdities, such as qualities existing apart from any subject. The Pythagoreans, finally, are praised for reaching beyond sensible nature to mathematical principles, yet criticized because such abstract, unmoved objects cannot explain motion, generation, or the physical differences among bodies.",
  "B1C9": "This chapter contains Aristotle's extended case against the Platonic Forms, and Aquinas organizes his defense along several lines. First, positing the Forms does not explain sensible things but merely doubles them, since for every kind of thing one now needs both the sensible instance and a separate Form of the same name - like someone unable to count a few things who tries to count by adding more. Second, none of the standard arguments for the Forms actually proves their existence: the argument from the sciences would generate Forms even of things nobody wants Forms of; the 'one over many' argument would yield Forms of mere negations; the argument that thought survives its object's destruction would give Forms even of perishable things. Third, and most fundamentally, Aquinas stresses that the Forms do nothing for the things they are supposed to explain. They cannot be the source of a sensible thing's motion or change, since they are themselves changeless; they are not needed for scientific knowledge, since the mind grasps universal natures through abstraction without requiring a separately existing counterpart; and calling sensible things 'copies' that 'participate in' or 'imitate' the Forms is, Aquinas notes, empty metaphor rather than genuine causal explanation, since one thing can resemble another without literally being modeled on it. Even the Phaedo's claim that the Forms cause both being and becoming fails, because something must still move a thing into existence at a given time, and the Forms, being immobile, cannot supply that efficient causality. Finally, where Plato identifies the Forms with numbers, further difficulties arise, since things identical in kind cannot be the cause of one another; and throughout, Aquinas underscores that by omitting efficient and final causality altogether, Plato reduces the natural, ever-changing world almost to the fixed status of mathematical objects.",
  "B1C10": "Aquinas reads Aristotle's closing judgment as a summary verdict on the entire history just reviewed: the early philosophers, between them, touched on all four causes, but only obscurely and piecemeal, like children just learning to speak, who stammer and lisp before they can talk properly. As his example, Aristotle points to Empedocles, who came remarkably close to a genuine insight when he said that bone exists by virtue of a certain ratio, or proportion, among the elements mixed into it - which is really nothing other than bone's formal cause or essence, the answer to what bone most basically is. Yet Aquinas notes that Empedocles never followed this insight through: he did not consistently apply the same reasoning to flesh and the other bodily tissues, and he never clearly recognized that it is this formal proportion, and not simply the raw material elements he named, that truly accounts for why a thing is what it is. Aquinas treats this as emblematic of the whole tradition surveyed in the book: real intuitions of formal, efficient, and final causality appear here and there among the ancients, but always half-formed and never expressed with the clarity that Aristotle's own fourfold analysis achieves. The chapter, and with it the historical survey of Book One, closes by looking forward: Aristotle promises to return to the specific difficulties, or aporiai, raised by these earlier opinions later in the work, since working through such puzzles carefully will help advance the inquiry toward the truth about first causes and principles that the whole science of wisdom is seeking.",
  "B10C1": "Aquinas opens by locating this book within the larger project: having treated being in its various senses, he now turns to unity, which is convertible with being itself. He sorts the primary senses of 'one' into four: what is continuous by nature (most fully so when its motion is single and undivided, as with a straight or circular line); what is a complete whole possessing one form, especially when nature rather than glue or nails holds it together; what is numerically singular and so cannot be said of many things; and what is one in intelligibility, grasped by a single undivided act of understanding, as a species is grasped despite being realized differently in many individuals. All four, he insists, reduce to a single underlying note: indivisibility. Yet he draws a careful distinction between the things that are called one and what 'oneness' itself means - much as fire truly is an element without 'element' naming fire's own essence, so unity names an attribute that indivisible things have, not a separate nature.\n\nAquinas then follows Aristotle into unity's role as measure. Because the one is indivisible, it is the standard by which quantity of any kind becomes knowable - first and most properly the numerical unit, from which the notion of measure spreads analogously to length, weight, and even speed, always seeking the smallest, least-divisible standard so that nothing added or subtracted escapes notice. He extends this by analogy to knowledge and sense, which are called measures of things known and perceived, even though, properly speaking, it is the object that measures our cognition, not the reverse - a point aimed squarely against Protagoras's claim that man is the measure of all things.",
  "B10C2": "Here Aquinas addresses whether unity is itself a self-standing substance, as the Pythagoreans and, following them, Plato supposed, or whether it is instead a property found in some underlying nature, as the early nature-philosophers assumed when they identified the ultimate 'one' with love, air, or the boundless. Aquinas sides decisively with the second view, tying it to a conclusion he had already established regarding substance: no universal can subsist on its own apart from the particulars it is said of, because a universal is by definition common to many, and what is common cannot also be a separate singular thing. Since being and unity are themselves the most universal predicates of all, extending to every category, they especially cannot name subsisting substances or constitute a genus in their own right. Aquinas develops this through Aristotle's illustrations from color, music, and speech: were all beings simply colors, they would form a number of colors and their 'one' would be some particular color, namely white; the same logic holds for tones and letters. The lesson generalizes to substance itself - in each category one must ask what the underlying one actually is, since 'being one' never suffices as a nature in its own right. Aquinas closes by clarifying the relation between unity and being: they are really the same subject, differing only in intelligibility, since unity adds nothing but the negation of division to being. He uses this occasion to correct Avicenna, who mistakenly treated being and unity as added accidental features of things, having been misled by the equivalent term 'one' that functions as a countable unit within the genus of quantity - a specialized sense distinct from the unity convertible with being itself.",
  "B10C3": "Aquinas explains that unity and multitude are opposed most properly as the indivisible to the divisible, a relation he classifies among the contraries rather than among contradictories or correlatives, even though it carries a privative character, since 'one' is named from the negation of division found in its opposite. He notes an important asymmetry: multitude and divisibility are, in the order of our sense perception, more evident and so linguistically prior, which is why the one takes its very name and definition from what it negates - yet in the order of nature and reality, oneness remains prior, since indivisibility is presupposed by any division. From this basic opposition, Aquinas traces a family of related pairs. Sameness, likeness, and equality all cluster on the side of unity, while otherness, unlikeness, and inequality cluster on the side of multitude. He carefully distinguishes several senses of 'the same' - numerically identical, one in both form and matter, or one merely in the account of primary essence, as with equal lines. He is equally attentive to 'otherness': things differ absolutely when nothing about them is shared, but 'difference' proper is narrower still, requiring some underlying sameness - a shared genus or species - within which the differing items diverge. Difference, in other words, presupposes an identity that otherness as such does not. This sets up the book's central claim, which Aquinas signals here: that contrariety is itself a species of difference, indeed its most complete form, since contraries are precisely those things that differ to the greatest possible degree while still sharing a common genus.",
  "B10C4": "Aquinas shows that wherever more and less can be found, a maximum must also be found, since an endless ascent is impossible; and because some things differ from others more than others do, there must be some pair that differs the most. This maximal difference he identifies with contrariety. He argues that things differing merely in genus cannot supply this maximum, since they are simply incomparable, admitting no scale of more or less; only things differing in species, where a genuine process of change runs from one extreme through intermediates to the other, allow for a true maximum - the two outermost termini of that process. Because the maximal and the complete coincide (nothing lies beyond either), contrariety is also perfect difference. From this Aquinas draws the corollary that a single thing can have only one contrary, since a single distance can have only two extremes, and he shows how this one definition underlies the four traditional descriptions of contraries offered by other philosophers - greatest difference, greatest difference within a genus, within the same recipient matter, and within the same science. He then turns to contrariety's root: privation and possession. Not every privation is a true contrary, only a 'perfect' one that reaches maximal distance from its corresponding possession - mere absence of color is not blackness's opposite to whiteness in the relevant sense. Aquinas carefully distinguishes contrariety from contradiction: contradiction is immediate and admits no middle term whatsoever, while contrariety, resting on privation attached to a determinate subject, characteristically does admit intermediates. Since generation and change move between contrary extremes, one member of every contrary pair must involve some privation, and ultimately all contrariety traces back to the primal opposition between the one and the many.",
  "B10C5": "Aquinas takes up a genuine puzzle: if each thing has exactly one contrary, how can 'equal' seem opposed to both 'great' and 'small' at once, an arrangement that would seemingly violate that rule? He walks through the arguments on both sides. Some evidence suggests equal really does stand contrary to the great and small together, since we naturally ask 'whether' something is greater, smaller, or equal, and the word 'whether' properly signals opposition. Yet equal cannot be simply contrary to one of the pair alone, since there is no more reason to pair it with the great than the small, and it cannot be contrary to both together either, since that would mean one thing having two contraries, which is impossible; besides, equal functions as an intermediate between great and small, and contrariety, being maximal difference, admits no intermediate standing between itself and either extreme. Aquinas resolves this by denying that the opposition is contrariety at all: equal opposes the great and the small jointly as a privative negation, not as a contrary. It denies both excess and deficiency, but only within things naturally suited to have size at all - which is why the negation carries the sense of privation rather than a bare logical negation applicable to anything whatsoever. Because this joint denial falls upon one single, unified subject (quantity), it acquires its own name, 'equal,' unlike the analogous case of 'neither good nor bad,' which remains unnamed because goodness and badness admit no single unified underlying subject. This is also why equal serves as a genuine intermediate between the extremes, while some privative middles, lacking a single receptive nature, never received one manageable name at all.",
  "B10C6": "Aquinas addresses a parallel puzzle about how the one is opposed to the many. If they were opposed without qualification, absurdities follow: since 'many' is opposed to 'few,' the one would have to count as few; and since two counts as 'many' in the sense that it is already a multiple, one would seem forced into the position of being a kind of plurality - which is impossible, since one is precisely what is not divided into a plurality. Aquinas untangles this by distinguishing two senses of 'many.' In one sense, it signifies an excessive plurality relative to some standard, opposed properly to 'few,' which signifies a deficient plurality; in this sense even 'much water,' a continuous quantity, can be called abundant without being 'many things.' In the other, stricter sense, 'many' simply means number as such, a plurality measured by the unit, and in this sense it is opposed only to the one, as the measured is opposed to its measure, not as a contrary. Two therefore counts as 'many' in this second, absolute sense, since it already constitutes a number, yet remains the very first and smallest instance of 'few,' since nothing is more minimal than two - one itself is not properly 'few' at all, only the ultimate measuring unit. On this basis Aquinas corrects Anaxagoras, who spoke of all things existing together, unbounded in both plurality and smallness: he ought instead to have said 'fewness,' since smallness (a continuous quantity) can regress infinitely by division, but fewness among discrete things cannot, having its natural floor at two. The one and the many, Aquinas concludes, are related as measure to measurable - a relative opposition, not a strict contrariety.",
  "B10C7": "Aquinas explains why genuine intermediates must exist between contraries and how they are constituted. First, every intermediate belongs to the same genus as the extremes it stands between, since change from one thing to another - passing necessarily through whatever is intermediate - occurs only within a single genus; one cannot pass from color to shape except incidentally. Second, intermediates arise strictly between opposites of the contrary type: contradictories, admitting no middle at all, allow none; nor do non-reciprocal relatives such as knowledge and the knowable, since these do not share one genus. Only true contraries, like the great and the small, or colors ranging between white and black, properly admit a mean. Aquinas then works out the heart of the argument: intermediates are literally composed of the extremes. Since a genus is divided into contrary species by contrary differentiae - white and black, for instance, being distinguished as colors that respectively disperse or concentrate sight - those constitutive differentiae are themselves prior contraries. Any intermediate species, such as the colors lying between white and black, must likewise be composed from the genus and some differentiae, but these cannot simply be the same extreme differentiae (or every color would just be white or black); rather, they must be intermediate differentiae, sharing in the two primary contrary differentiae to varying, gradated degrees. Something that has more of one extreme's character and less of the other's is, in that very fact, compounded out of both extremes. Aquinas concludes that this holds throughout the whole hierarchy: every species within a genus, whether an extreme or an intermediate, ultimately traces back to and is composed from the primary contraries that first divide that genus.",
  "B10C8": "Aquinas turns to what makes things differ in species, arguing that specific difference always presupposes an underlying sameness of genus: things other in species must nonetheless share one identical common nature, which is then differentiated in a non-accidental way in each - not merely that both are, say, animals, but that 'animality' itself is realized differently as equinity in the horse and humanity in the man. He explains carefully how genus relates to differentia: unlike matter, which is only part of the composite it underlies, genus is predicated of the whole species, being denominated from what is material in the thing, just as differentia is denominated from its formal principle, even though both genus and differentia, properly speaking, characterize the whole. Against Plato, who treated universals as separately subsisting realities, Aquinas insists that the common nature itself is diversified through its differentiae - 'animal' just is, in itself, differently realized as horse and as man. From this Aquinas concludes that specific difference simply is contrariety: since division of a genus can occur only through opposites, and neither contradictory nor privative nor non-mutual relative oppositions can divide a genus into coordinate species, only contrariety remains capable of doing so. A corollary follows: nothing is properly said to be the same or different in species with respect to its own genus, since genus, taken by itself, contains no differentiating contrariety at all - genus and differentia relate as an indeterminate, matter-like principle to something further needed to complete it, not as one species relates to a rival species within that same genus.",
  "B10C9": "Aquinas confronts a natural difficulty: if contrariety is what makes things differ in species, why do male and female not constitute different species, given that masculine and feminine are genuinely contrary and belong to animal precisely as animal, not as an incidental attribute like paleness or darkness? He generalizes the puzzle - why does one contrariety, such as footed versus winged, produce specific difference, while another, such as pale versus dark, does not? His answer turns on the distinction between form and matter within a composite thing. Contrarieties located on the side of the formal principle, the very account or definition of a thing, do produce difference in species, since they diversify what a thing essentially is. Contrarieties located on the side of matter, however, do not, because matter individuates without altering the specific nature realized in it - a pale man and a dark man remain one species of man, since paleness belongs to this or that individual man materially considered, not to humanity as such. Aquinas applies this directly to sex: although male and female are proper, non-accidental differences of animal, they arise not from any difference in the animal's substantial form but from the disposition of its matter, namely the body, as shown by the fact that the very same seed becomes male or female depending on whether the vital heat acting upon it is strong or weak. Since one identical formal principle can issue in either outcome depending only on material conditions, sex cannot be a formal, species-determining difference, however genuinely it belongs to animal by nature rather than by accident.",
  "B10C10": "Aquinas closes the book by showing that the perishable and the imperishable, unlike male and female, do differ in genus and not merely in species. His reasoning rests on the analysis of potency and privation developed earlier: incapacity is a form of privation, and privation grounds contrariety, so perishable and imperishable, opposed as a determinate capacity and its corresponding incapacity, are genuine contraries. But whereas contrarieties rooted in form and act (like the differentiae dividing a genus into species) produce merely specific difference, contrarieties rooted in potency - which pertains to matter, the principle from which genus itself is drawn - produce difference in genus. Perishability and imperishability, moreover, belong to their subjects necessarily and per se, not accidentally as paleness or darkness belong to a man; nothing can be perishable now and imperishable later, the way one man can be pale now and dark later, since what is accidental is by nature separable from its subject, while what is essentially corruptible cannot at some point cease being so without ceasing to be at all. Aquinas draws out the corollary Aristotle intends: this rules out the Platonic doctrine of separately subsisting Forms. If a sensible, perishable man and his supposed imperishable Form were truly the same in species, as the Platonists claimed, one man would simultaneously be both perishable and imperishable - an impossibility once perishability is recognized as a necessary, essential feature rather than an accident. Since things differing in genus are further apart than things merely differing in species, and since the corruptible and incorruptible differ in genus, no sensible thing and its purported eternal Form can share so much as one common species, let alone one identical individual nature.",
  "B11C1": "Aquinas reads this chapter as a set of preparatory doubts that the science of wisdom must clear away about itself before it can proceed to its proper subject, separate substance. He organizes the puzzles under three headings. First, is wisdom a single science, given that it studies several first principles which, unlike the objects of other sciences, are not themselves contraries? Aquinas answers that wisdom remains one science because its many principles are all reducible to a single genus, namely being; this is the same reasoning by which a science of contraries counts as one, since contraries too share a genus. Second, does wisdom examine the common principles used in demonstration, such as 'the whole is greater than its part'? Aquinas says yes, and principally so, because it is wisdom's business to know the common notions - being, non-being, whole, part - that underlie such principles, and the particular sciences merely borrow this knowledge from it. Third, does wisdom study all substances, and does it treat accidents as well as substances? Aquinas holds that it studies all substances insofar as they share the one genus of being-through-itself, though separate, immaterial substances receive its principal attention, and it treats accidents only in reference to the substances of which they are demonstrated. He also addresses whether wisdom concerns the four causes treated in physics, denying that mathematical objects or Platonic Forms exist as separate entities, and closes by weighing whether genera or species better deserve the name of principle - concluding that genera, being simpler and containing many species potentially, have the better claim, even though species are in another sense more indivisible.",
  "B11C2": "Continuing the survey of unresolved doubts, Aquinas here treats questions about what wisdom's subject-matter actually consists in. He first asks whether anything exists beyond singular sensible things for this science to consider, since a science of pure individuals is impossible - individuals being infinite in number and therefore unknowable scientifically. His resolution is characteristically careful: nothing exists in reality apart from singulars themselves, but the intellect can and does abstract common natures from them, and it is these universal natures, not free-standing entities, that intelligible science grasps. He then asks the more pressing question whether any substance actually separate from sensible things exists. Aquinas affirms that such substances do exist, but he is emphatic that they are not the separated Forms or Ideas of sensible kinds that Plato imagined; rather, as he will show later in the work, they are the first unmoved movers, and only these deserve the name. He works through the further puzzle of whether matter or form has the better claim to be a first principle, concluding that since matter is only ever in potency while form is actuality, form is by nature prior - yet sensible forms are themselves corruptible, so an eternal separate substance is still needed to explain why the world exhibits lasting order at all. Aquinas also rejects the idea that unity and being function as genera, since no differentia can be said to 'participate' in them, and dismisses attempts to make points, lines, or numerical units into self-subsisting principles, since these are merely divisions or limits of continuous magnitudes and cannot exist apart from what they limit.",
  "B11C3": "Here Aquinas explains how a single science can study something said in many senses. He notes that if 'being' were purely equivocal, with nothing shared across its uses, no one science could investigate it, since a science requires a genuine common subject. But being is not spoken of that way; it resembles terms like 'medical' or 'healthy', which are used of many different things - an instrument, a diet, a complexion - not because they share one definition, but because each is related in its own way back to a single reference point, namely medicine or health. Aquinas calls this kind of unity analogical, standing midway between strict univocity, where a term means exactly the same thing in every application, and sheer equivocity, where it means something wholly different each time. Being works analogically in just this fashion: substance is called being in the primary sense, because it has existence properly in itself, while quality, quantity, and the other categories are called beings only because they are dispositions or determinations of substance. Because everything reduces to this one focal meaning, and because every real contrariety likewise reduces to a first contrariety within being, philosophy - unlike physics, which studies things as movable, or dialectic and sophistic, which trade in the same terms without penetrating to being as such - can treat all beings as belonging to one science. Aquinas also clarifies an apparent problem about privative opposites that admit an intermediate, such as just and unjust: the privation here removes only the complete, perfect degree of the positive term, not its entire meaning, which is why partial failures in obedience to law can occupy a middle ground.",
  "B11C4": "Aquinas shows here how first philosophy relates to mathematics and physics by comparing how each uses the common axioms of demonstration, such as the principle that equals taken from equals leave equals. The mathematician relies on this axiom constantly but only within his own restricted domain, applying it to lines, numbers, or angles considered strictly as continuous quantities, without regard to whether these quantities exist as beings in the fuller sense. It belongs to first philosophy, by contrast, to consider such common axioms in their full universality, as they hold of anything whatsoever insofar as it is a being, not merely insofar as it is quantified. Aquinas draws the same comparison with natural science: physics studies the principles and properties of the things that are, but only insofar as they are subject to motion, whereas the primary science studies the same underlying subjects insofar as they simply exist, prescinding from the character of being movable. Because of this difference in the formal object under which each science considers its material, both mathematics and physics turn out to be, in a sense, particular or partial sciences falling under the single universal science of wisdom, which alone examines being as being and therefore alone can adjudicate on the principles that every subordinate science silently presupposes. This is why, Aquinas notes, it belongs properly to the philosopher rather than to the mathematician to investigate the ultimate justification of these common axioms.",
  "B11C5": "Aquinas here treats the principle that the same thing cannot both be and not be in the same respect at the same time, which he identifies as the first and most certain of all principles precisely because its terms, being and non-being, are the first things grasped by the intellect. Because nothing is more basic from which it could be derived, this principle cannot be demonstrated in the strict sense; it can only be defended 'ad hominem', by compelling an opponent who denies it into self-contradiction. Aquinas lays out three lines of argument used for this purpose. First, meaningful speech requires that a word signify one determinate thing; anyone who says 'this is and is not' has, by uttering the negation, unsaid what his own affirmation just signified, which destroys the possibility of communication itself. Second, if a statement is true it signifies something with a certain necessity, and what is necessarily so cannot simultaneously fail to be so; hence contradictory statements about the same subject cannot both be true. Third, if affirmation and its contradictory negation were equally true, no statement would be any more accurate than its opposite, collapsing all distinctions - one could then say with equal justice that a man is a horse. Aquinas notes that these arguments are aimed specifically at Heraclitus, who held that opposites can be simultaneously true of the same thing, and observes that Heraclitus adopted this position without grasping its full consequences, since even the claim that opposites can both be true would, by its own logic, be no truer than its denial, destroying rational discourse altogether.",
  "B11C6": "Aquinas turns next to Protagoras's dictum that man is the measure of all things, which he treats as a variant of the error just rebutted, since it implies that whatever appears to someone is true for that person, so that contradictory judgments about the same thing could both stand. He traces this opinion to two sources: the natural philosophers' assumption that nothing can come to be from what simply is not, which seemed to force one to say a thing must already be both white and not-white before it changes; and the observation that different people judge the same things differently, as tastes seem pleasant to some and unpleasant to others. Aquinas resolves the first difficulty by recalling the distinction between existing potentially and existing actually - what becomes white comes from what is only potentially white, not from sheer non-being. Against the second, he insists it is childish to weigh every appearance as equally authoritative, since the same object never seems sweet to one and bitter to another unless a sense organ has been damaged; the untainted judgment is the true measure. He adds further considerations: the heavenly bodies remain constant and provide a fixed standard for truth; motion presupposes something moved from a determinate start to a determinate end, impossible if contradictories held simultaneously; medical and dietary practice assumes real, stable natures in things; and if only human perception seems unstable, this reflects our own condition, not flux in the world. Aquinas concludes that neither Heraclitus nor Anaxagoras, who held everything is present actually in everything, can be right, since both views make contraries predicable of the same subject at once.",
  "B11C7": "Aquinas compares wisdom to the other theoretical sciences along three lines. First, regarding how each science obtains its basic definitions: every particular science assumes rather than demonstrates the essence of its subject, arriving at it either through sense observation or by borrowing it from a higher science. Natural philosophy studies mobile things whose definitions include sensible matter; mathematics studies things that, while existing in sensible matter, can be defined without reference to it, since their properties do not depend on matter being of any particular sensible kind; wisdom alone studies substances that are wholly separate from matter and immobile. Second, among the theoretical sciences - physics, mathematics, and what Aquinas calls theology or divine science - theology ranks highest in nobility, because a science's dignity follows the dignity of its object, and the separate, immaterial substances that theology studies are the most excellent of existing things. Third, Aquinas explains why wisdom alone, among the theoretical sciences, can be called universal: physics and mathematics each mark off one determinate class of being for themselves, whereas wisdom considers being qua being and the first principles from which every particular being and its own principles ultimately derive, making it prior to and more comprehensive than the others. He also revisits the classic example distinguishing 'snub', whose definition necessarily includes matter (a snub nose), from 'concave', whose definition does not, applying this distinction to show that the natural philosopher must define things like flesh or the eye with reference to their matter, while wisdom's separate substances require no such reference at all.",
  "B11C8": "Aquinas explains here why there can be no science of accidental being, that is, of what happens neither always, nor for the most part, nor by any determinate necessity, but merely by chance. No recognized art or science concerns itself with such accidents: architecture does not consider whether the inhabitants of a house will be happy there, and no craft studies what merely happens to coincide in a subject without following from its essential nature. Only sophistic exploits this kind of accidental predication, which is why, Aquinas notes, Plato was right to say the sophist trades in a kind of non-being. Aquinas gives the reason no science can grasp such things: every science concerns what is invariable or at least regular, but the accidental by definition escapes both categories, arising from no determinate or ordered cause. He adds an important argument: if accidental events had determinate causes in the way essential effects do, then tracing any causal chain back far enough would show that everything happens of necessity, eliminating chance and contingency from the world altogether - an unacceptable conclusion. Aquinas then distinguishes accidental being from two other extended senses of 'being': being as truth or falsity, which is merely a construction of the mind in judging by combining or dividing concepts, and therefore not something wisdom investigates as a reality outside the mind; and being by chance or fortune, which he defines as an accidental cause operating within events that are ordered toward an end by nature or by deliberate purpose. Because fortune presupposes purposeful or natural causation and is not itself a per se cause of anything, Aquinas concludes that accidental causes, including fortune, are always posterior to and dependent upon essential causes such as reason and nature.",
  "B11C9": "Aquinas here works through Aristotle's definition of motion, one of the more difficult notions in philosophy. He sets out several premises: being divides into potency and actuality; being divides into the ten categories; motion does not constitute some further, eleventh category, but tracks the same division of potency and act found within each category; and within any genus - substance, quality, quantity, place - one finds both a complete, perfected state and an incomplete, imperfect one. On this basis Aquinas explains motion as the actuality of what exists potentially, precisely insofar as it is still potential - neither the bare potency itself, since the potential as such need not be undergoing anything, nor the fully realized actuality that terminates the process, since once the house exists building has ceased, but the incomplete actuality lying between the two, occurring exactly while the potential is being brought toward its term. He illustrates this with bronze, which is potentially a statue: the actuality of the bronze precisely as bronze is not motion, but its actuality precisely as buildable-toward-something-else is. This is why motion is so hard to classify, and why others mistakenly called it a kind of otherness, inequality, or indeterminacy: it is neither pure potency nor complete actuality, but a real hybrid of the two. Finally, motion resides properly in the thing moved rather than in the mover, since the actuality of the power to move and the actuality of what is moved are, in underlying reality, one and the same process, differing only in the respect under which each is considered - much as the road from Thebes to Athens and from Athens to Thebes is a single road under two descriptions.",
  "B11C10": "Turning to the infinite, a property attaching to motion and to quantity generally, Aquinas first distinguishes four senses of the untraversable or infinite: what is by its very kind incapable of being traversed at all, as a point cannot, in the way a sound cannot be seen; what has only begun to be traversed and not yet completed; what could in principle be traversed but so vastly exceeds any traverser's actual power that it is infinite for practical purposes, like the depth of the sea; and what is naturally suited to have a limit but simply lacks one, which alone is the infinite in the strict sense. He also distinguishes infinity by addition, as with number, to which a unit can always be added, from infinity by division, as with continuous magnitude, always further divisible, noting time and motion are infinite both ways at once. Aquinas then reports and endorses arguments that no actually infinite substance can exist, separate as the Platonists supposed or inhering in a sensible body as the early naturalists held: an infinite substance, having no quantity or plurality of its own, would have to be indivisible, yet the genuinely infinite must be quantified, a contradiction; and if divisible, every part of it would itself have to be infinite, impossible since a whole is always greater than its part. He further rehearses the naturalistic arguments against any actually infinite sensible body, composite (since its elements are finite in number and must remain proportionate to preserve the mixture) or simple (since each element has a determinate active power, and an infinite element would overpower and destroy the others), concluding that no sensible or intelligible body, and no separately existing number, can actually be infinite.",
  "B11C11": "Aquinas here classifies the different ways something can be said to change. Change occurs accidentally when something changes only because something associated with it changes, as when 'the musician walks' or a sailor is said to move because his ship moves; it occurs per se but only partially when a part of a thing changes, as a body is healed because its eye is healed; and it occurs per se and primarily when the whole subject itself is directly moved, as when a stone falls. Aquinas draws the identical threefold distinction on the side of the mover. He then identifies five elements present in every change - the primary mover, the thing moved, the time of the change, and the two termini, that from which and that to which it proceeds - observing that change is properly differentiated by these termini rather than by mover, subject, or time, which are common to every change. Working through the possible combinations of positive and negative termini, Aquinas shows that change from a negative state to nothing in particular is no real change, leaving three genuine types: change from a negative term to its positive contradictory, which is generation; change from a positive term to its negative contradictory, which is destruction; and change between two positive contrary or intermediate terms, which alone deserves the name motion in the strict sense. He explains why generation and destruction are not themselves motion: since non-being cannot properly be said to be moved, and what comes to be is, in the relevant respect, not yet in being, generation cannot be classified as a movement, though it remains a real kind of change alongside motion proper.",
  "B11C12": "Aquinas here explains why motion in the strict sense can occur only in three categories - quality, quantity, and place. Since motion always runs between contraries or their intermediates, and only these three categories admit true contrariety of a determinate, continuous kind, there can be no motion in substance (only generation and corruption, whose termini are contradictories, not contraries), in relation (change occurs there only accidentally, when a correlative shifts because its partner changes), or in action and passion. Aquinas addresses the seeming counterexample of fire and water, which appear contrary as substances; he rejects treating heat and cold as themselves the substantial forms of these elements, and rejects letting substantial forms admit degrees like accidents, concluding they are contrary only in the qualified sense that one implies the intelligible privation of another, as animate opposes inanimate. He then gives several arguments against 'motion of motion' or 'becoming of becoming', among them that this would make motion its own subject of further change, and that it would generate an infinite regress with no first term, leaving nothing able to come to be at all. Aquinas closes by distinguishing three senses of 'immobile' - what cannot possibly be moved, as God; what could be moved only with great difficulty; and what is naturally movable but simply is not moving under its proper conditions, which alone is properly called rest, since rest is the privation of motion in a subject capable of motion. He then defines the spatial relations structuring local motion - together, apart, contact, betweenness, contrariety of place, succession, contiguity, continuity - closing by distinguishing the point from the numerical unit, since points admit contact and always have something between any two, while units, related only by succession, do not.",
  "B12C1": "Aquinas opens by explaining why this book turns to substance as the proper object of first philosophy. Everything else predicated of a thing—quality, quantity, relation, and the rest—exists only because a substance exists to bear it; strip away substance and nothing else remains standing. This is why the earlier natural philosophers, in searching for principles and causes, were in fact always searching for the principles of substances, even when they didn't say so. Aquinas then follows Aristotle in sorting substance into three kinds: sensible substances subject to generation and corruption (plants, animals, the ordinary things around us), sensible substances that are eternal though still bodily (the heavenly bodies, which move but do not come to be or perish), and a third kind that is wholly separate from matter and motion altogether. He is careful about the status of this third kind: earlier thinkers who posited it—those who spoke of separate Forms, or of mathematical objects existing on their own—disagreed about how many such immaterial realities there are and how they relate to sensible things. Aquinas notes that the first two kinds of substance fall under natural philosophy, since both involve motion in some way, while the immaterial kind, if it exists, requires a distinct science to study it—namely, the one this whole treatise pursues. His point is architectonic: the entire investigation of Book Twelve is set up as an ascent from the substances we know through the senses toward one that is not sensible at all, and this ascent is what justifies metaphysics as a science in its own right.",
  "B12C2": "Turning to sensible substance, Aquinas walks through Aristotle's account of change as requiring three things: a subject that persists, and two contrary terms, one lost and one gained. Since the contraries themselves cannot survive the change (whiteness does not become blackness), something else must underlie both—this is matter, understood not as a third \"thing\" alongside form and privation but as the potency that can be informed first one way and then another. Aquinas stresses the technical point that grounds the whole chapter: everything that comes to be, comes to be from what is potentially but not yet actually what it becomes, not from sheer nothingness. This lets Aquinas credit the older physicists, notably Anaxagoras with his talk of an original \"all things together,\" with having groped toward the notion of potency without ever formulating it properly—their mixture-doctrines are best read charitably as confused approaches to prime matter and potentiality. He also lingers on the puzzle of how something can come from \"non-being,\" distinguishing the different senses of that phrase so that generation is not made to look like the production of being from absolute nothing, which would violate the principle that nothing comes from nothing without qualification. Aquinas closes by drawing the chapter's threefold conclusion: every changeable substance is analyzed by three principles—matter as the substrate, form (or the privation of form) as the term reached or lost. This threefold analysis becomes the tool Aquinas will use throughout the rest of the book to distinguish sensible substances from the immaterial substance still to come.",
  "B12C3": "Here Aquinas addresses a subtlety that will matter greatly for his own theology later: neither ultimate matter nor substantial form, strictly speaking, \"comes to be.\" What comes to be is always the composite—this bronze sphere, this man—not the bronze or the sphere-shape taken by themselves, since positing generation of the principles themselves would send the analysis into an infinite regress with no stopping point. Aquinas also develops Aristotle's rule that generation proceeds from an agent bearing the same specific form as the effect: a man is begotten by a man, not produced from an abstract universal \"humanity.\" This becomes the springboard for a direct critique of Plato's separately subsisting Forms. If what actually causes a new individual to exist is always another concrete individual of the same kind, acting through its own form, there is no need to posit a separately existing Form of Man floating apart from particular men to explain why men generate men; the form already at work in the begetting parent does the job the Platonic Idea was invented for. Aquinas does allow one significant exception, which he flags for later development: some forms may persist after the composite dissolves, most notably the human intellective soul, since its characteristic operation, understanding, does not depend intrinsically on the body's matter in the way sensation does. He leaves it an open question whether the whole soul survives or only its intellectual part. The chapter overall establishes matter, privation, and form as adequate principles of sensible substance without recourse to separated universals.",
  "B12C4": "Aquinas takes up the question of whether all things—substances, qualities, relations, quantities—share the very same principles, or only analogous ones. He rejects strict identity: an element cannot literally be shared across radically different categories, since an element must be simpler than and different from what it constitutes, and no single item could function as literally identical matter for both a substance and, say, a relation. Instead Aquinas explains Aristotle's more careful answer: causes are the same only \"by analogy,\" not univocally. Within one genus, such as sensible bodies, things do share literally common principles (hot and cold as formal principles, an underlying subject as matter); but across radically different genera, the sameness is only proportional—as form, privation, and matter stand to color, so they stand, proportionally, to day and night, light and darkness and air serving as the analogous triad. Aquinas draws out the deeper lesson: besides the intrinsic principles (matter and form) constituting a thing, there is always also an extrinsic moving cause, something already actual that brings the change about, and this fourth item—the efficient cause—is what ties every particular chain of change back, ultimately, to something first. This chapter thus quietly sets up the entire argument of the book: analogical causal structure across all beings points toward a single ultimate mover standing behind the diversified causal orders, glimpsed here through the recurring figure of \"that which first of all moves all things.\"",
  "B12C5": "Building on the analogy of causes, Aquinas develops the pairing of act and potency as principles that cut across every category of being, more fundamental even than matter and form themselves. The same subject, such as wine or flesh, can be actually one thing and potentially another at different times; but Aquinas is careful to note a further complication—sometimes cause and effect do not share matter in the same way, as when a father generates a son (an external per se cause) while the sun's motion along the ecliptic also contributes causally to generation and corruption in the sublunary world, without being the son's matter or form at all. Aquinas draws special attention to the principle that universal causes, strictly speaking, do not exist as separate agents: it is always an individual that causes an individual—this man, not \"man in general,\" begets that man, just as your particular father, not fatherhood as such, caused you. This is another quiet rebuttal of Platonism, denying that a separately subsisting universal could do a singular thing's causal work. At the same time Aquinas shows how, despite this radical individuality of efficient causation, one can still speak truly and universally about causes in the abstract, since matter, form, privation, and mover remain the same in kind across individuals of a species even though numerically different in each. The chapter closes his survey of sensible substance and its principles, preparing the transition to the immaterial substance argued for in what follows.",
  "B12C6": "This is the chapter's pivotal argument, and Aquinas gives it careful attention. Because motion and time can neither have begun nor can they cease—there could be no \"before\" without time already presupposed—there must exist some substance capable of sustaining eternal motion. Aquinas insists this cause cannot be merely potential, a substance that merely could move things but might not; potency by itself explains nothing necessary, since what is merely potential need not ever be actualized. Hence there must be some principle whose very essence is actuality itself, with no unrealized potency in it at all—which is also why Aquinas concludes this substance must be immaterial, since matter is precisely the principle of potentiality. He addresses a natural objection: doesn't potency always precede act, since a thing must first be able to act before it does? Aquinas, following Aristotle, answers that while in any single changeable thing potency for a given act may temporally precede its exercise, actuality is absolutely and simply prior in the order of causality, since only something already actual can bring another thing from potency into act. Notably, Aquinas here reads Aristotle as a philosopher arguing strictly from natural reason about motion's past eternity, distinguishing this philosophical conclusion from the truth of creation in time, which Aquinas elsewhere holds as an article of Catholic faith not owed to natural demonstration. The chapter ends by affirming that a pure, eternally active first cause—rather than \"chaos\" or the mythical accounts of the poets—best explains why some things move in one fixed way, and others vary.",
  "B12C7": "Having proved a first unmoved mover must exist, Aquinas now explains how it moves without itself being moved: it acts as an object of desire and understanding, not as a mechanical push. Ordinary movers act by contact and are themselves altered in acting; but the first mover causes motion the way something loved moves a lover, purely as a final cause, drawing the heavens' desire and intellect toward itself without undergoing any change in the process. Aquinas develops the identification of this substance's activity with pure intellection: since God's substance is not composite, his understanding and his being are one, and since only the noblest object is fit for the noblest act of understanding, God's thinking has no object but himself—thought thinking itself. Aquinas underscores that this self-contemplation is also, properly speaking, life in the fullest sense, since life consists in immanent activity, and intellectual activity is life's highest form; hence God is rightly called a living, eternal, and supremely good being. He then refutes Speusippus and the Pythagoreans, who held that perfection appears only at the end of a process (as fruit follows seed) rather than at the beginning. Aquinas insists this gets causality backward: whatever produces something must already actually possess, in a higher way, the perfection it communicates—the seed itself only exists because a prior complete organism produced it. The chapter closes by establishing that this first substance has no magnitude, being incapable of the finite power a body would entail, and is wholly impassible and unchangeable, since even alteration presupposes the more basic reality of spatial motion, which this substance transcends entirely.",
  "B12C8": "Aquinas now asks whether one such immaterial substance suffices or whether many must be posited, and here his surviving commentary becomes especially rich before it is cut short. His reasoning: each distinct eternal circular motion observed in the heavens—not only that of the outermost sphere but each planet's proper motion—requires its own proportionate, eternal, unmoved cause, since an eternal effect needs an eternal cause suited to it. Since astronomy detects multiple independent celestial motions, there must be a corresponding plurality of separate immaterial substances, one for each. Aquinas patiently works through the specific models of Eudoxus and Callippus, tallying the concentric spheres each assigns to the sun, moon, and planets, to arrive at a working total for how many unmoved movers exist beyond the first—while candidly noting, as Aristotle himself does, that the precise number is a matter for astronomers to determine more exactly and remains provisional. As his exposition proceeds, Aquinas introduces later Arabic commentary into the discussion, weighing Avicenna's view on whether the outermost heaven is moved immediately by the first principle itself or rather by a subordinate intelligence caused by it—a distinction Aquinas elsewhere uses to relate Aristotle's plural unmoved movers to the angelic intelligences of Christian doctrine, without simply equating the two. It is in the middle of unpacking this very question, weighing how much plurality of causality such subordinate movers introduce, that the text of Aquinas's commentary breaks off, apparently mid-sentence, before the chapter's own argument for the unity of the physical universe and its concluding remarks on the ancient mythical tradition about the gods is ever reached.",
  "B12C9": "Aquinas's commentary breaks off before reaching this chapter; no surviving commentary from him exists on this passage.",
  "B12C10": "Aquinas's commentary breaks off before reaching this chapter; no surviving commentary from him exists on this passage.",
  "B13C1": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle begins his examination of mathematical objects by first recalling the doctrine of sensible substances established earlier. He distinguishes two contrary opinions: one that mathematical objects exist as separate substances (the Platonic view), and another that they exist only in sensible particulars. Aquinas would approve Aristotle's rejection of Platonic mathematical Forms as self-subsisting entities. Mathematical objects are universal abstractions—lines, numbers, surfaces—that the intellect derives from sensible matter through a process of separation or precision (abstractio). They lack independent existence; they are beings of reason insofar as they are considered apart from the material singulars that instantiate them. Their truth consists in their correspondence to intelligible structure within created things, not in a transcendent realm. Aristotle's critique of the Platonists—that if mathematical objects subsisted separately, they could not account for the sensible world—reflects Aquinas's conviction that only God subsists apart from created multiplicity. All true being flows from act-as-such, and mathematical abstractions depend entirely on the actualized particulars from which intellect knows them.",
  "B13C2": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nHere Aristotle examines whether mathematical objects, even if they exist, could be separate substances. Aquinas reads this as a dialectical dismantling of the Platonic position. If numbers and geometric forms were truly separate from sensible bodies, they would need their own principles of being and change—yet they possess no such principles. Numbers do not come into being or pass away (at least not in the way sensible things do), nor do they interact causally with the physical world. This silence reveals the absurdity of the Platonic thesis. In Aquinas's terms: mathematical objects are indeed eternal and unchanging—precisely because they are not things-that-are-in-act in the strong sense. Rather, they represent structures *discovered* in being, patterns that intellect grasps when it abstracts from matter. The eternal character of mathematics thus reflects not a separate realm but the necessity of the logical relations among universals, which flow necessarily from the intelligible essences actualized in the sensible creation. To posit them as separate substances would multiply beings without necessity, violating the principle that being should not be predicated equivocally.",
  "B13C3": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle pushes deeper: can mathematical objects be both universal *and* separate substances? For if they are universal (predicated of many particulars), how could they exist apart as individual substances? Conversely, if they are individual substances, how are they universal? This contradiction, Aquinas observes, follows necessarily from the Platonic error. It exposes the incoherence of the view that mathematical universals are self-subsisting things. Aquinas's own position is consonant here: universality belongs to concepts in the intellect and to natures considered as principles of predication—not to substances *as such*. A universal substance would be a contradiction in terms, a confusion of the order of being (ens) and the order of predicability (secundum praedicationem). Mathematical objects are universal in intellect because they abstract from singularizing matter; but their being—insofar as they have any being outside the mind—is grounded in the particulars that exemplify their structures. This chapter crystallizes why a medieval Christian metaphysician must reject the Platonic divinization of mathematical abstractions.",
  "B13C4": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle examines the generation and corruptibility of mathematical objects. They appear neither to come into being nor to perish in any natural sense. But this, he suggests, is because they are not substances at all, only abstractions of the intellect. Generation and corruption belong to substances in act, which undergo motion and change through the agency of causes. Mathematical truths—that 2+2=4, that the angles of a triangle sum to 180°—do not come-to-be through any efficient cause; they are grasped as necessary consequences of the essences involved. Aquinas would emphasize that this timeless necessity of mathematical truth constitutes no argument for their subsistence. Rather, it shows that mathematics deals with intelligible essences, not with singular existents. The intellect knows these essences truly, but their truth is a truth *in the mind* (veritas in mente) reflecting structures present in the real world, not a proof of their independent being. The immutability of mathematical objects thus reflects the immutability of the divine intellect's knowing, in which all such truths are eternally present as divine ideas—not Plato's Forms, but God's exemplary knowledge.",
  "B13C5": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nHere the question becomes: if mathematical objects are neither substances nor generated, what is their ontological status? Aristotle clarifies that they are indeed real—there is genuine knowledge of them—but they exist *in sensible particulars*, abstracted by intellect. A line is not a self-subsisting entity; it is what intellect apprehends when it considers the extension of a sensible body. The universal applies to all such cases through the unity of the intelligible form. Aquinas finds here a vindication of his own doctrine of the universale in re (the universal existing in the thing). A nature or essence—even a mathematical one—has a real foundation in the particular, though its universality is a work of intellect. The ontology is: sensible substance (ens in actu); its intelligible structure (apprehended by abstraction); and the universal concept (formed in the intellect). None of these levels is false; all correspond to the graduated hierarchy of being that flows from God. Mathematical truth thus participates in the immutable truth of the divine mind, yet depends wholly on the creation of sensible particulars.",
  "B13C6": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle turns to the question of divisibility and unity in mathematical objects. A line can be divided, yet we speak of unity in numbers and figures. How are these reconciled? The resolution lies in understanding what \"unity\" and \"divisibility\" mean at each level. In the sensible particular, continuous quantity admits of division without destruction of substance (a rod can be cut into pieces, each still real). In the mathematical abstraction, divisibility is a *conceptual* feature—the line is divisible in thought, meaning we can consider parts of it while grasping it as one form. This unity-in-divisibility reflects, in Aquinas's view, the deeper metaphysical truth that being itself embraces both unity and multiplicity, act and potentiality. God is pure Unity (actus purus), and all created being exhibits the tension between act (the unifying form) and potentiality (the divisible matter or substrate). Mathematics crystallizes this tension in intelligible form, showing how multiplicity and unity cohere. This chapter thus elevates mathematical discourse to a mirror of metaphysical structure.",
  "B13C7": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle addresses the question of whether mathematical objects can be said to have matter. If they do, it would be intelligible matter, not sensible matter. Intelligible matter is the quantity or extension considered apart from sensible qualities—what remains when intellect abstracts from color, hardness, etc., focusing only on shape and size. Aquinas recognizes this doctrine of intelligible matter (materia intelligibilis) as a refinement crucial to understanding how abstraction works. Sensible particulars possess sensible matter (this wood, this bronze). When intellect abstracts, retaining quantity but setting aside sensible accidents, it grasps intelligible matter. Mathematical objects are thus forms of intelligible matter: a triangle in pure geometry is a form imposed on the intelligible matter of extension. This preserves the Aristotelian insight that even universals and abstractions have a quasi-material component (a principle of multiplicity) preventing them from collapsing into pure form. Once again, Aquinas sees the graduated ontology: sensible matter → intelligible matter → pure form (God). All reflect the fundamental act-potency structure of all being-other-than-God.",
  "B13C8": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nThe question now: is a number one substance, or many substances? If the number \"five\" is a substance (the Pythagorean view), is it one substance or five? The incoherence reveals another angle of the Platonic error. Aquinas would note that this puzzle dissolves once we recognize that \"five\" is not a substance at all but a *universal concept*. The numeral five is one universal (the form or essence of fiveness) predicated of many quintuples, or—in a different sense—the unity of a single concept in the mind. Substance is always particular and concrete. Numbers abstract from singularity, so they cannot be substances. Yet numbers are *real* as objective logical relations grounded in created beings. The existence of five sheep demonstrates the real applicability of the number-concept; the concept \"fiveness\" is not a mere subjective invention but captures an intelligible feature of the sensible world. This chapter shows that Aristotle (and Aquinas) can grant full reality to mathematical knowledge without hypostatizing mathematics into a realm of Platonic Forms.",
  "B13C9": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle examines the claim that points, lines, and surfaces are substances or components of substances. But these geometrical abstractions depend on sensible bodies for their intelligibility. A point exists only as the limit of a line; a line only as the limit of a surface; surfaces only as the bounds of bodies. Remove the sensible body (or at least the intellectual consideration of it), and these lower-dimensional entities have no independent being. This reflects Aquinas's hierarchy: a geometric point is less a being than the line that bounds it, which is less a being than the surface, which is less a being than the three-dimensional body. All are abstractions from the lowest level of abstraction—pure potentiality, or intelligible matter—which grounds them all. This exemplifies the general truth that in the order of abstract knowledge, the more abstract (the point) depends on the less abstract (the body), just as the accidental depends on the substantial. God alone is utterly independent; all creation exhibits this web of dependence, intelligibly reflected in the hierarchy of mathematical abstractions.",
  "B13C10": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle concludes his analysis by affirming that mathematical objects, precisely because they are not substances, are secondary realities—beings-by-abstraction, not beings-in-act. Yet they are not false or merely subjective. They represent intelligible patterns in the sensible world, knowable with certainty because they capture necessary truths about quantity and structure that inhere in all created bodies. For Aquinas, this vindication of mathematical knowledge without Platonic realism completes the metaphysical vision: sensible substances are primary, containing within them intelligible essences; through abstraction, intellect grasps these essences as universals; mathematical objects are the purest expression of intelligible quantity, expressing truths that flow necessarily from the essences actualized in creation. This entire structure of knowing—from the sensible singular to the abstract universal to the intelligible necessity—reflects the overflow of divine wisdom into the creation. Mathematics thus becomes a hymn to the rational order God has inscribed in being itself.",
  "B14C1": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle revisits the nature of unity and being, having closed his discussion of mathematical objects. Unity and being are coextensive: whatever is, is one. But \"one\" admits of many senses, as does \"being\"—they are said analogically. Aquinas deeply approves this analogical approach, for it resolves the apparent paradox that being is both utterly simple (a fundamental transcendental convertible with unity itself) and infinitely diverse (appearing in infinite forms in creation). God is pure being, absolutely simple, purely one. All creatures have being by participation; in each, act and potentiality are distinct, so unity is achieved through form imposing order on matter. Unity in creatures is therefore never absolute or unqualified, but always qualified by the degree of actualization and the composition present. This chapter sets the stage for a metaphysical re-examination of how unity and being relate in the caused realm, reaffirming that the analogy of being is the master-key to all philosophical understanding.",
  "B14C2": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nThe discussion turns to the distinction between unity-as-continuity (in substances, especially bodies) and unity-as-identity (in concepts and universals). A human being is one through the continuous unity of matter and form, through the actual integration of potentialities into a single substantial form. Yet the concept \"human\" is also one—the universal shared by many particulars. Aquinas sees both as real: the unity of the substance is a unity-in-act (unum secundum actum), grounded in the substantial form; the unity of the universal is a unity-of-concept (unum in ratione), which is \"one\" as an object of intellection but exists in many (vel in multis, according to the maxim). The metaphysical mystery deepens: how can the one universal truly be in many? Through the act-potency structure: the nature (human-ness) is potential to instantiation in many particulars; it is actualized variously in each. This chapter crystallizes the medieval recovery of Aristotelian realism against both nominalist dissolution and Platonic reification of universals.",
  "B14C3": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle addresses the unity of substance versus the unity of the universal, pushing harder. Is the principle of unity in a substance (this man) the same as the principle of unity in the concept \"man\"? No, replies Aristotle (with Aquinas in full accord). In the individual substance, unity comes from the substantial form that actualizes prime matter and integrates all accidents into a single being. In the universal, unity comes from the intellect's grasping of a common nature. Both unities are real, but their grounds are different. This distinction is vital to Aquinas's entire metaphysics: it prevents the confusion of the transcendental order (where being and unity reign) with the conceptual order (where universals reside). A failure to distinguish leads either to nominalism (denying real universals) or to Platonism (making universals into separate substances). The via media of Aristotelian-Thomistic realism holds fast: real essences in things, abstracted by intellect into universal concepts, both genuinely real at their respective levels.",
  "B14C4": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nThe question of numerical identity becomes acute. Is the universal \"Socrates\" (his individual nature considered as a principle of his properties) the same as \"Socrates\" (the concrete existent individual)? In one sense yes: they are identified in reality. In another sense no: the universal is considered apart from particularity, while the existent is singular. Aquinas's doctrine of the compositionality of creature-being resolves this: Socrates is a composite of essence and existence (essentia et existentia). His essence—what makes him human, his humanity—is a universal principle (capable, in itself, of being in multiple instances). But this essence is *this* essence, actualized in *this* existent by a unique act of being (actus essendi). Thus identity and distinction cohere: Socrates's individual being is the actualization of a unique universal essence, united uniquely to an act-of-being that is his alone. This intricate metaphysics explains how the eternal Forms of Plato are unnecessary; God, knowing all essences eternally in His infinite intellect, creates a universe of singular instances, each a unique hypostasis of an intelligible nature.",
  "B14C5": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle explores whether contrariety and opposition illuminate the nature of being and unity. Contraries (hot/cold, large/small) are linked by a common genus or subject; they are not simple opposites but opposites *within* a field of potentiality. Aquinas recognizes here the profound insight that being-and-not-being, act-and-potency, are not brute opposites. Rather, potency is a real principle, a \"not-yet-actualized\" capacity oriented toward act. Contraries in the sensible world reflect this fundamental structure: they arise in a common subject (a body can be hot or cold) and represent different actualizations of a shared potentiality. Contrariety thus bespeaks the metaphysical composition of all creatures: they are potentiality seeking actualization through form. Even at the level of being itself, an analysis of contrariety illuminates how creation is neither absolute non-being (which cannot have contraries) nor the infinite undifferentiated being of the divine nature (which knows no contrariety), but a realm of participative being where multiplicity and change are grounded in act-potency. This chapter shows why opposition, properly understood, reveals the metaphysical structure of all that-is-not-God.",
  "B14C6": "[AI-generated synthesis in the Thomistic tradition — Aquinas did not comment on this book. This summary is composed by Claude based on Aquinas's known philosophical positions, not sourced from his writings.]\n\nAristotle concludes by affirming that the science of being-qua-being (metaphysics) has analyzed the deepest structures of all that exists. Unity, being, substance, cause, act-potency—these form the irreducible framework in which all intelligibility resides. For Aquinas, this represents philosophy's highest achievement: to articulate the conceptual architecture of reality itself, showing how all things flow from the absolutely simple, self-subsisting being of God, and how all created being exhibits the fundamental structure of composition, dependence, and participation. The entire Metaphysics culminates here: from the wonder at sensible things that sparked inquiry (Book I), through the systematic analysis of causation, substance, and form, to the recognition that all coherence, unity, and intelligibility derive from the transcendent One. Metaphysics thus becomes a philosophical hymn to the Creator, even if Aristotle himself did not name Him as such. For Christian Aquinas, this Aristotelian metaphysics provides the rational foundation upon which revealed theology—the knowledge of God in His inner life, His creation, His redemption—can be erected without contradiction, fulfilling the truth that faith seeks understanding.",
  "B2C1": "Aquinas reads this opening chapter as Aristotle's account of how the mind stands toward the highest truths, the ones metaphysics itself pursues. The inquiry is easy in one sense: everyone, by natural light, grasps certain basic and self-evident starting points, so that no one is wholly shut out from truth, the way no one can wholly miss a doorway open to all. Yet it is hard in another sense, because possessing the whole of truth, or hitting the precise part one is aiming at, exceeds what any single mind can manage on its own; only the community of thinkers across time approaches completeness, each contributing a portion. Aquinas locates the deeper source of this difficulty not in the truths themselves but in the disproportion between our intellect and them: our understanding is naturally fitted to material, sensible things, and only weakly and derivatively able to reach immaterial and separate realities, much as the eye of a bat is overwhelmed by daylight. He also takes the occasion to reject the view, associated with Averroes, that human beings can attain full, connatural knowledge of separate substances in this life. Because earlier thinkers, even when mistaken, sharpened the tools of inquiry and prepared the way for those who came after, gratitude is owed to all of them, not only to those we agree with. Aquinas then explains why metaphysics deserves the name \"knowledge of truth\" above the other sciences: theoretical knowledge has truth itself as its very end, while practical knowledge subordinates truth to action; and since whatever most fully causes a quality in other things possesses that quality most eminently, the first and most universal causes of things must also be supremely and unqualifiedly true, more true than anything caused by them.",
  "B2C2": "Here Aquinas follows Aristotle's demonstration that causes cannot proceed to infinity, treating the argument as applying, in turn, to every kind of cause distinguished in his philosophy: efficient, material, final, and formal. For efficient and moving causes, he reasons that if a series had no first member, every member would be a mere intermediate; but an intermediate cause only transmits the causality of something prior to it, so that if nothing were first, nothing could really explain anything, and the whole series would collapse into unintelligibility. For material causes, Aquinas carefully distinguishes two ways one thing is said to come from another: as the man comes from the boy, where the later state is the fulfillment of a potency already underway in the earlier, and as one element is generated from the corruption of another, where the coming-to-be of one is strictly the passing-away of its predecessor. Neither pattern, he argues, tolerates an infinite regress, since each requires a determinate boundary or terminus to make sense of the process at all; there must be some primary matter or ultimate subject beneath the changes. Turning to final causality, Aquinas insists that if ends receded infinitely, the very notion of a good or a purpose would be destroyed, since something is called good precisely as a terminus that other things are for; and no agent, natural or intellectual, could act at all if it had no determinate end in view, since purposeful motion requires a limit toward which it tends. Finally, regarding formal causes and definitions, Aquinas explains that a definition cannot be endlessly resolved into further defining terms, because prior, more universal terms already contain and explain what is proper to what follows; an infinite regress here would make science and definition impossible, since nothing could ever be fully grasped or known if understanding always awaited one more term.",
  "B2C3": "In this final chapter Aquinas takes up Aristotle's remarks on method, reading them as a caution about the proper way to receive philosophical teaching. He observes that a listener's habituation shapes what kind of exposition seems fitting to him, since long custom comes to function almost like a second nature, forming an expectation of how truth ought to be presented. This is why, he notes, even fabulous or childish elements retained in old laws can carry more persuasive weight with the untrained than sound reasoning does, simply because people are accustomed to them. Different audiences, accordingly, demand different modes of proof: some trust only mathematical demonstration, others need concrete examples, others look to cite poets or established authorities as witnesses, and still others insist on painstaking exactness in every point, while some are irritated by such exactness and regard it as needless fussiness, much as some regard scrupulous dealing in trade as somehow petty. Aquinas draws from this the methodological lesson that one must already be trained, through a prior grounding in logic, to recognize which kind of argument is proper to which kind of subject matter, since it is unreasonable to seek both the knowledge itself and the correct manner of pursuing it at the very same time. He then applies this principle directly to the limits of mathematical precision: such rigor is appropriate only where the subject matter is free of matter, that is, in immaterial or abstract objects, but cannot reasonably be demanded in natural philosophy, whose subject is bodies involving matter and therefore admits only conclusions that hold for the most part rather than with strict necessity.",
  "B3C1": "Aquinas opens by explaining why Aristotle refuses to settle anything before first laying out every difficulty the coming science must face. He gives four reasons drawn from Aristotle's own text. First, resolving a question later is nothing but untying a knot one has already examined, and no one can loosen a knot without first looking closely at how it is tied. Second, a mind caught in doubt is bound much as a body is bound by chains; just as a man with his feet tied cannot walk forward, a mind that has not faced its perplexities cannot advance toward the truth. Third, someone who has not marked out the difficulty beforehand is like a traveler with no fixed destination, unable to know whether he has arrived. Fourth, a good judge must hear the arguments on both sides of a dispute before deciding, and the reader of this science must do the same. Aquinas stresses that this is Aristotle's habitual practice throughout his works, but here it takes a distinctive form: rather than raising each doubt individually as a topic comes up, he gathers, in one sweep, every perplexity bearing on the whole science of first causes and universal being before beginning to answer any of them. What follows, then, is not confusion but a deliberate map of the terrain - the full set of questions concerning causes, principles, substance, unity, genera, and the perishable and imperishable - that the rest of the work will address one by one in proper order.",
  "B3C2": "Aquinas walks through several linked puzzles here. On whether one science can study all the causes at once, he notes the objection that immobile realities have no moving or final cause in the ordinary sense, since these involve change; yet he shows Aristotle's eventual answer is that wisdom studies form, end, and, where applicable, moving cause together, because these are not opposed to one another the way rival principles would be. On whether the common axioms, such as the impossibility of simultaneously affirming and denying the same thing, fall under this same science, Aquinas explains that they belong to it precisely because it studies being as such, and every axiom concerns being universally; other sciences merely borrow these axioms as already known, while the philosopher alone defends them against those who deny them, arguing dialectically rather than demonstrating them from anything prior. On whether one science treats all substances and their attributes together, Aquinas holds that metaphysics considers substance under its common character, and therefore also considers the attributes common to all being, even though it defines substance rather than demonstrating it, while it does demonstrate substance's proper attributes. Finally, on whether separate Forms and mathematical intermediates exist besides sensible things, Aquinas traces the Platonists' reasoning - a double abstraction, first of universal from particular, then of mathematical objects from sensible matter - and lays out Aristotle's objections: an eternal duplicate of a perishable man is absurd, and positing separate mathematical heavens or a separate medicine multiplies unwanted intermediates without warrant.",
  "B3C3": "Here Aquinas takes up two connected doubts about principles. The first asks whether genera - such as the broad category to which a thing belongs - are the true principles of things, or whether the ultimate parts composing a thing hold that role instead, as letters compose a syllable rather than the general notion of 'articulate sound' doing so. Aquinas resolves this by a distinction: genera do serve as principles of knowing, because a definition is built from genus and specific difference, and to define something is to know what it is. But being a principle of our knowledge is not the same as being a principle of a thing's actual being. What genuinely constitutes a thing in reality is closer to its concrete parts or its form and matter, not the abstract universal under which we classify it. The second doubt asks, granting that genera are somehow principles, whether the highest and most universal genera - being, unity, and the like - are more truly principles than the narrower genera predicated immediately of individuals. Aquinas notes that this question only has force for those, like the Platonists, who take universals to be separately subsisting things; on that assumption, the most universal reality would indeed be most fundamental. But once one denies that universals exist apart from the things they are said of, the specific and proximate forms - nearer to the individual - turn out to be the more genuine principles, since form generally causes and constitutes a thing more directly than remote, generic matter does.",
  "B3C4": "This chapter gathers Aquinas's treatment of four of the hardest aporiai. First, whether something exists beyond matter as a cause in its own right - some separate form. Aquinas notes the case for it: without an ungenerated principle, generation would either regress endlessly or never get started at all. Yet it is unclear where to stop positing such separate forms, since obviously no separate 'house' exists apart from particular houses; Aquinas signals that the eventual answer distinguishes immaterial substances, which really are separate, from the forms of material things, which never exist apart from matter even though the intellect can grasp them universally. Second, whether the principles of things are numerically one or only alike in kind; Aquinas points ahead to Book Twelve, where intrinsic principles like matter and form turn out to be the same across individuals only by analogy, while separate substances are each numerically singular. Third, whether perishable and imperishable things share the same principles. Aquinas carefully unpacks Aristotle's critique of the mythical theologians who made the gods immortal through eating nectar and ambrosia, and of Empedocles, whose strife and love cannot consistently explain why some things last forever and others do not. Fourth, whether being and unity are themselves the substance of things, as Plato and the Pythagoreans held, or instead qualities of some other underlying subject; Aquinas shows how both Parmenides's collapse of all things into one and Zeno's argument against indivisible unity create genuine difficulty, pointing forward to Aristotle's later teaching that a thing is one simply through its own essence, without needing a separately existing 'unity itself.'",
  "B3C5": "Aquinas here follows Aristotle's puzzle over whether numbers, solid bodies, surfaces, lines, and points are themselves substances. The case for treating them as such runs through elimination: qualities, relations, and quantities are all said of some underlying subject and are not themselves a 'this,' so if body, too, fails to qualify, nothing would remain to count as substance at all. Yet a further problem arises, because surface, line, and point seem in some ways more substantial than body itself, since body is bounded and defined by them while they do not depend on body for their own definition - a solid is understood through its surfaces, a surface through its lines, a line through its points. Against this, Aquinas explains Aristotle's counter-argument: a given shape, such as half of a cube, is only potentially, not actually, present within an undivided solid, much as a statue is only potentially present in an unworked block of stone; so surfaces, lines, and points exist in bodies merely potentially rather than as actual substantial parts. A further difficulty concerns generation: when bodies are joined or divided, their boundaries seem to come into being and pass away, yet without any of the ordinary marks of generation or corruption, since there is no matter out of which a point or surface could be made or into which it could dissolve - much like the 'now' of time, which is never generated yet is never quite the same moment twice. Aquinas notes that Aristotle's eventual solution treats these mathematical entities not as substances but as accidents of quantity belonging to composite bodies.",
  "B3C6": "Aquinas closes the book's survey of doubts with three further questions. First, why posit separate Forms at all, once sensible things and mathematical intermediates are already granted? He reconstructs the Platonist reasoning: just as many individual sensible men share one specific nature without being numerically one, so too many equal triangles or lines share one mathematical species without being numerically identical; if that is all there is, then the principles of things would be determinate only in kind and never in number, which the Platonists found intolerable, and so they added the Forms to secure something genuinely one in number beneath the many. Aquinas notes Aristotle's eventual reply that moving causes are indeed numerically determinate, while formal principles shared by a species are rightly determinate only in kind, so no separate Form is actually needed. Second, whether the elements or principles of things exist potentially or in some other, actual way; Aquinas shows the difficulty runs both directions, since positing them as already actual seems to require something still prior, while treating them as merely potential seems to make nothing actually exist at all, and he points ahead to the resolution that act is simply prior to potency, so that a first principle must exist actually. Third, whether principles are universal or individual; universals, predicated commonly, seem unable to be a 'this' and so unable to be substances, yet only the universal is properly an object of scientific knowledge, leaving the puzzle Aristotle later untangles by distinguishing subsisting singulars from the universal manner in which the intellect knows them.",
  "B4C1": "Aristotle opens by declaring that there is a science whose object is being as being, not this or that restricted portion of being, as the particular sciences study. Aquinas explains that every particular science - mathematics, natural philosophy - cuts off some segment of reality and investigates only the properties peculiar to that segment; none of them asks what belongs to a thing simply because it is a being at all. This new science, then, must have being itself as its subject, and must consider not only that subject but whatever attaches to it in its own right, per se, rather than merely incidentally. Aquinas draws an analogy to geometry: the geometer does not care whether a triangle happens to be made of bronze or wood, only with what follows necessarily from its being triangular; likewise the metaphysician sets aside what merely happens to belong to things and confines himself to what follows from being as such. He further connects this to the ancient search for first principles and highest causes: whoever sought the ultimate elements of things was implicitly seeking the principles of being as being, since nothing is more universal than being itself. Hence the science that investigates first causes and the science that investigates being qua being turn out to be one and the same undertaking - this is the long-sought science that the whole treatise calls first philosophy or wisdom, and Aristotle here simply establishes that it exists and names its proper subject.",
  "B4C2": "Aquinas reads this long chapter as showing, step by step, exactly what being as being covers and why a single science can treat so vast a subject. He begins from Aristotle's illustration involving the word healthy: diet, medicine, urine, and a living body are all called healthy, yet not in one identical sense - the diet preserves health, the medicine produces it, the urine signals it, the body possesses it. These different senses are nevertheless all ordered toward one reference point, health itself. Aquinas calls this pattern analogical predication, distinct both from univocal predication, where a term keeps one shared meaning, and from pure equivocation, where meanings are unrelated. Being works the same way: substance, quantity, quality, motion, privation, and negation are all called beings, but only because each bears some relation to substance, which is being in the primary sense. Because one science can study everything ordered toward a single primary term, first philosophy can study all these modes together, though its principal concern remains substance. Aquinas then shows that being and one name the same reality under different aspects - convertible terms, unity adding only the note of undividedness to being - and concludes that wherever being has distinct species, such as substance, quantity, and quality, unity has corresponding species, such as sameness, equality, and likeness. Since one science studies a subject together with its essential attributes, and since plurality, sameness, otherness, and contrariety all flow necessarily from being and unity, the philosopher must examine these together with the negations and privations bound up with them. Aquinas also explains how dialectic and sophistic each imitate philosophy's universal reach, dialectic by arguing probably about everything and sophistic by merely appearing wise, and takes this resemblance as confirming that being itself is truly the philosopher's proper and singular subject.",
  "B4C3": "Aquinas takes Aristotle's question here to be whether the axioms employed in every demonstration, and above all the principle that a thing cannot both be and not be in the same respect at once, belong to this same universal science or fall instead to some more restricted discipline, such as mathematics, which relies on them constantly. He argues that since these axioms hold true of everything that is, without being confined to any single genus of being, they must belong to whoever studies being universally, namely the metaphysician; the particular sciences merely borrow and apply them within their own limited subject matter, never pausing to demonstrate them. Aquinas notes that certain natural philosophers wrongly took themselves to be the final authorities on such principles, mistakenly supposing that changeable, natural substance was the whole of reality; but because metaphysics considers a wider and higher kind of being than physics does, it is metaphysics, not physics, that has the truer claim to examine these common truths. Aquinas then identifies the principle of non-contradiction as the firmest of all such axioms, and explains why: no one can genuinely be deceived about it, since deception occurs only where something is not already known; it does not rest on any prior supposition, since it must be possessed by anyone who understands anything whatsoever; and it arises naturally within the intellect, through the mere grasp of the terms involved, rather than through acquired demonstration. Every other axiom, and indeed every act of reasoning, ultimately rests upon it as its final and most certain foundation.",
  "B4C4": "Here Aristotle confronts those who, following Heraclitus and others, hold that contradictory statements can be true of the same subject at the same time. Aquinas insists that this claim cannot be demonstrated false in the strict sense, since any such demonstration would already presuppose the very principle under dispute; instead Aristotle proceeds refutatively, forcing the opponent into self-contradiction, on the sole condition that the opponent's words mean something definite. Aquinas traces several distinct lines of attack in turn. First, if a name like man signifies one definite thing, then to be a man cannot also be to be a non-man, since a thing cannot simultaneously possess and lack its own defining essence; this shows that predication must sometimes be essential and not merely accidental, since purely accidental predication running to infinity would leave nothing fixed to talk about at all. Second, if affirmation and negation were both true of everything indifferently, then all distinct things, a man, a wall, a ship, would collapse into one indistinguishable reality, since nothing could be truly said of one that could not equally be said of any other. Aquinas connects this directly to Protagoras's claim that whatever appears true to someone simply is true, and to Anaxagoras's doctrine that all things are mixed together, arguing that both dissolve the very possibility of determinate knowledge. Later, Aquinas develops what he treats as a distinct argument from degrees of falsity: even an opponent must admit that some errors lie closer to the truth than others, since claiming four equals five is less mistaken than claiming four equals a thousand, and this graded nearness to truth is unintelligible unless something is simply and unqualifiedly true. Throughout, Aquinas stresses that the denial of this principle, followed out consistently, would make rational speech, judgment, and purposeful action alike impossible.",
  "B4C5": "Aquinas connects this chapter to the same root error examined earlier, tracing it now to two sources: the doctrine of Protagoras, that whatever seems true to a person simply is true, and a further argument drawn from observing nature itself. He explains that some thinkers were led to deny fixed truth through genuine philosophical difficulty, upon noticing that contrary qualities, hot and cold for instance, seem to arise out of one and the same underlying thing, which suggested to Anaxagoras and Democritus that opposites must already be present together beforehand. Aquinas resolves the puzzle by distinguishing potential from actual being: a thing can indeed be potentially both hot and cold at once, as lukewarm water is, but never actually both together, so the appearance of contradiction dissolves once potency and act are properly distinguished. He also notes Aristotle's appeal to an unchanging, immaterial reality as proof that not everything is caught up in such flux. Aquinas then surveys why sense experience misled Empedocles, Parmenides, Anaxagoras, and, in the poetic testimony of Homer's wounded Hector, why identifying knowledge outright with bodily sensation led these thinkers to conclude that truth itself shifts as the knower's bodily condition shifts. Aquinas rejects this by insisting that sensation is not identical with intellectual judgment, and that the object producing a sensation exists in itself prior to, and independently of, being sensed. He likewise turns aside the extreme Heraclitean position associated with Cratylus, who thought change so total that nothing could truly be named at all, by observing that even a changing subject retains something enduring throughout its change, and that only a small portion of the physical world, not the whole of reality, is actually subject to constant generation and decay.",
  "B4C6": "Aquinas reads this chapter as Aristotle's answer to a further sophistical challenge: since perceptions and judgments differ between the sick and the healthy, the wise and the foolish, the sleeping and the waking, who has the right to say which perception is the true one? Aquinas observes that such questions, though posed as serious puzzles, are really no different from idly wondering whether one is presently asleep or awake, and that those who press this line demand a reason for everything, failing to see that not every truth needs, or even admits, demonstration; some truths are simply the starting points from which every demonstration must proceed. He explains Aristotle's central move: not everything that exists is relative to some perceiver's opinion, so the claim that whatever appears is true cannot stand in that unqualified form. One may only say that a thing appears true to a particular perceiver, in a particular respect, under particular conditions, at a particular time; once such qualifications are supplied, the apparent contradiction disappears, since what looks like honey to sight but not to taste involves two distinct judgments about two distinct respects, not one judgment contradicting itself. Aquinas adds that if everything truly depended on being perceived or thought, nothing could have existed before any perceiver did, which is plainly false. He closes by drawing out the conclusion toward which the whole discussion has been building: since contradictory statements cannot both be true of the same subject at once, neither can genuine contraries belong to the same subject at the same time and in the same respect, though they may belong to it in different respects, or one actually and the other only potentially.",
  "B4C7": "Turning from the principle of non-contradiction to the closely related principle of excluded middle, Aquinas explains that Aristotle now argues no third possibility can stand between an affirmation and its contradictory negation: of any given subject, one must either truly assert or truly deny a given predicate, with nothing in between. Aquinas lays out several of Aristotle's proofs. From the very definitions of true and false: to say of what is that it is, or of what is not that it is not, is true, and nothing besides these two options is available, so any statement about a thing must be simply true or simply false. Aquinas also distinguishes two ways something might function as a middle term - one, like grey between black and white, sharing in both extremes within a common genus, and another, like a stone standing between man and horse, belonging to neither extreme and to no relevant common genus at all. He shows that neither kind of middle can be inserted between a contradictory affirmation and its negation: were it of the first sort, change could never pass directly between the two extremes without first passing through the middle, contrary to what is actually observed; were it of the second sort, no coherent account of change could be given at all. Aquinas notes that Aristotle traces the two mistaken positions back to their philosophical roots: Heraclitus's teaching that all things simultaneously are and are not tends to make everything true, while Anaxagoras's notion of a universal mixture, which posits a middle between contradictories, tends rather to make everything false, since a true mixture, being neither one extreme nor the other, cannot be accurately described by either.",
  "B4C8": "In this closing chapter, Aquinas sees Aristotle gathering the threads of the whole book against two remaining extreme positions: that nothing whatsoever is true, and that everything without exception is true. Aquinas shows how each position collapses under its own weight the moment it is stated, since whoever claims that everything is true thereby affirms as true the further claim that his own view is false, and whoever claims that nothing is true thereby admits that his own claim is false as well. Even if such thinkers try to exempt their own assertion from the universal rule, Aquinas notes that Aristotle drives them into an unending regress of further exemptions, since calling one statement true generates the further true statement that it is true, and so on without limit. Aquinas traces both extreme positions back to Heraclitus, who held that all things are perpetually in motion, and follows Aristotle's rebuttal that if literally everything moved and nothing whatsoever remained fixed, nothing could be truly said of anything, since what is true already is the case, whereas what is merely coming to be is not yet; universal flux would thus produce, absurdly, universal falsehood. Yet Aquinas equally rejects the opposite extreme, that all things simply rest, since the truth-value of statements plainly does change over time, as when a person who now utters a sentence once did not exist to utter it. Aquinas closes by underscoring Aristotle's resolution: reality is neither wholly at rest nor wholly in motion, nor does everything merely alternate between the two, because there exists something always in motion and, beyond that, a first mover which is itself entirely unmoved - a conclusion Aquinas reads as pointing toward the higher, immaterial causes that first philosophy investigates more fully in the books that follow.",
  "B5C1": "Aquinas opens Book Five by observing that a principle and a cause are, in reality, the same underlying thing, though the two words differ in what they signify: 'principle' names an order of before and after, while 'cause' names an actual influence on the being of what it causes. Because order is most familiar to us in local motion, Aquinas traces how 'beginning' first meant the point in a magnitude or path from which motion starts, then, by extension, the point from which learning or any process proceeds most conveniently, which need not be the point that is first by nature. He works outward through further senses: the intrinsic first part from which a thing is generated (a ship's keel, a house's foundation, the heart or brain in an animal), the extrinsic source of a change (a parent, an insult that starts a quarrel), that whose will governs what is moved (magistrates, the ruling or 'architectonic' arts that command subordinate ones), and finally that from which a thing first becomes known, as the premises are the beginning of a demonstration. All these converge on one common note: a beginning is simply whatever is first, whether in being, in becoming, or in being known. Aquinas adds that some beginnings are intrinsic to the thing, such as nature, matter, and form, while others are extrinsic, such as intellect, will, and the end pursued, which moves both action and understanding.",
  "B5C2": "Aquinas presents the four causes Aristotle distinguishes and stresses that the final cause required deliberate proof, since earlier natural philosophers had overlooked it entirely. Matter is that intrinsic, persisting stuff out of which something comes to be, such as bronze remaining bronze even after becoming a statue. Form is the pattern or definition that makes a thing what it is, whether as intrinsic shape or as an exemplar to be imitated. The efficient cause is the source from which change or rest first begins, and Aquinas here inserts Avicenna's fourfold division of agency into what perfects, what merely disposes matter, what assists a principal cause, and what supplies the very end and form of acting, as an architect does for a shipbuilder. The final cause is that for whose sake something occurs, and Aquinas shows this genuinely counts as a cause because 'why' questions are answered by naming it. He then draws out several consequences: several per se causes, not merely coincidental ones, can belong to one effect, since matter and agent cause differently; causes can be reciprocally causal, as exercise and good bodily condition are each cause of the other in different respects; and one and the same thing, through its presence or absence, can be cause of contrary outcomes, as a helmsman's presence brings safety and his absence brings wreck. Efficient and final causes correspond to each other, as do matter and form, each pair causing the other's being in a different way.",
  "B5C3": "Having distinguished the four kinds of cause, Aquinas next shows that all the ways a cause is named reduce to those four, since letters cause syllables, matter causes artifacts, simple bodies cause mixtures, parts cause wholes, and premises cause conclusions, all in the manner of material causation, while other examples fall under the other three kinds. He then draws the further distinction between species of cause and modes of cause: species divide according to essentially different ways of causing, while modes concern the varying relations a cause of one and the same kind can bear to its effect. Aquinas lays out these modes in pairs: prior and posterior causes, as a remote universal cause like the sun stands behind a proximate particular cause like a father; per se causes as opposed to accidental ones, since Polyclitus causes the statue only because he happens to be its sculptor; and simple causes as opposed to composite ones, where several elements are named together. He notes, too, that accidental causation itself admits of degrees of remoteness, and that potential causes differ from actual causes in that particular actual causes exist and perish together with their effects, while causes taken according to potency need not. Multiplying these pairs yields, Aquinas explains, twelve distinct ways causes may be considered, even though the fundamental kinds of cause remain only four.",
  "B5C4": "Aquinas draws four conditions from Aristotle's account of what makes something an element. First, an element must be a cause in the manner of intrinsic matter, so it belongs to material causation. Second, it must be that from which a thing is composed as its first and primary constituent, which distinguishes true elements from intermediate materials like bronze, itself made from something more basic. Third, an element must remain intrinsically present within the thing composed of it, unlike a contrary or privation from which something is merely said to come. Fourth, it must possess a single, indivisible species, so that however it is further divided, its parts remain of the same kind, as any portion of water is still water, unlike a syllable whose letters differ from one another. Aquinas illustrates this fourfold notion through letters as elements of speech, simple bodies as elements of mixtures, and, in demonstrations, the primary three-term syllogisms in which no middle term is drawn from a prior syllogism. He then explains a transferred, looser use of 'element' for whatever is one, small, and useful for many purposes, which led some thinkers to call the most universal things, such as genus, unity, or the point, elements. Aquinas notes this transferred usage is imprecise, since universals are not material components out of which particulars are built but rather predicate the substance of many things at once.",
  "B5C5": "Aquinas explains that Aristotle traces 'nature' back to the birth or generation of living things, from which the word itself derives, and observes that strictly speaking only living things are said to be born, so this original sense applies properly to plants and animals and their parts. From this first meaning follow four more: the intrinsic principle from which growth first proceeds; the intrinsic source of motion present in a natural thing according to its own essence and not incidentally, as the physician's art is not the source of his own recovery insofar as he happens to be sick; the underlying matter, unshaped in itself, out of which natural things are made, which some early philosophers mistakenly took to be the only true nature; and the form or essence, since nothing is said to have attained its nature until it possesses its proper shape, form being the terminus toward which generation moves. Aquinas is careful to insist that nature, on every one of these senses, always names an intrinsic principle, never an external one. He concludes that nature in the primary and most proper sense is the form or essence of things that have within themselves, as such, a source of motion, while matter is called nature only because it is receptive of form, and generation only because it is a movement proceeding from and toward form.",
  "B5C6": "Aquinas distinguishes four senses of the necessary. First, that without which a thing cannot live or continue to be, as breathing and food are necessary to an animal, functioning as a kind of contributing cause even though not the principal cause of life. Second, that without which some good cannot be attained or some evil avoided, as taking medicine is necessary for recovering health. Third, the compulsory or violent, whatever impedes a thing's own impulse or the will's own proposal, which is why necessity of this kind is called painful, since it opposes what we want, and also blameless, since we are only reproached for what we do willingly. Fourth, and most properly, that which cannot be otherwise than it is, necessary absolutely rather than merely in some respect. Aquinas shows that the other three senses all trace back to this fourth: compulsion is necessary because the one compelled cannot act otherwise; the first two senses are necessary because, given a certain end, life or good cannot come about except through them. He notes that demonstration depends on this strict necessity, since a demonstrated conclusion admits no alternative only if its premises are themselves necessary, and effects cannot follow necessarily from causes that are not. What is necessary in the fullest sense must be simple, incapable of being otherwise in any respect, which is why nothing violent or unnatural can belong to eternal, unmoving substances.",
  "B5C7": "Aquinas first addresses unity that occurs by accident, as when 'musical' and 'Coriscus' are called one only because one happens to belong to the other, or when two accidents like 'musical' and 'just' are called one only because both happen to inhere in the same subject; such unity is never predicated universally, since accidents belong to individuals rather than to natures as such. He then turns to unity that belongs to things by their own nature, presenting five principal modes. Things are one by continuity, more truly so when the continuity is natural rather than produced by art, and a straight line is more fully one than a bent one, since only the straight line's motion is entirely simultaneous throughout. Things are one when their underlying subject does not differ in kind, whether considered as the nearest matter, like wine and water each being one, or as the ultimate matter into which many things are ultimately resolved, like all liquids being one as water. Things are one when they share a single genus divided by opposite differences, as man, horse, and dog are one as animals. Things are one when the definition expressing one thing's essence is inseparable from that of another. And things are most fully one, especially in the category of substance, when the very concept grasping their essence is utterly indivisible, admitting no composition at all, as with a point or with unity itself.",
  "B5C8": "Aquinas shows how the five modes of essential unity discussed previously all reduce to a single root: whatever lacks division is called one precisely insofar as it lacks it, whether that indivision concerns quantity alone, an underlying subject, a genus, a definition, or, most fully, the very act of understanding a thing's essence. He adds a further nuance beyond mere indivision: something counts as a genuine, complete whole, and hence most truly one, not merely by being continuous but by possessing a single determinate form requiring an ordered arrangement of parts, as the pieces of a shoe are not truly one unless assembled into the shape and function of a shoe; for this reason the circle, being both continuous and complete in itself, is the most perfectly one of all figures. Aquinas then explains that unity is the very principle of number, since the first measure by which anything in a class is known counts as that class's basic unit, though this unit differs across different genera, being indivisible sometimes in quantity, sometimes in kind. Finally he presents the logical division of unity into oneness of number (shared matter), of species (shared definition), of genus (shared mode of predication), and of analogy or proportion (a shared relation between different pairs of things), noting that each later kind is broader than and implied by, but does not imply, the one before it.",
  "B5C9": "Aquinas explains that being divides first into being per accidens and being per se, a division distinct from that between substance and accident, since even accidents themselves, considered absolutely, belong to being per se, while being per accidens concerns only the manner of predicating one thing of another, as when an accident is predicated of another accident, of its subject, or a subject of its accident, in every case because one thing merely happens to belong to another. Being per se, by contrast, corresponds exactly to the figures of predication, the categories, since being is said in as many ways as things are predicated, whether signifying substance, quality, quantity, or another mode; being cannot be a genus divided by differentiae, since nothing lies outside being to serve as such a differentia, so it is instead contracted to the different categories through the different ways of predicating that follow the different ways of being. Aquinas notes a further sense in which being and 'is' signify simply that a proposition is true, and not-being that it is false, a sense he calls posterior to and dependent on the first, since truth or falsity of speech follows from how things actually stand. Finally, being and its parts divide according to potency and actuality, since a thing may be said to be, or to have some attribute, either potentially or in complete actuality, as an image is potentially present in unshaped stone.",
  "B5C10": "Aquinas presents four senses of substance drawn from common usage and philosophical opinion. First, the simple bodies and everything composed of them, animals, their organs, and even the beings some ancients revered as divine, all called substances because they are never predicated of a further subject while other things are predicated of them. Second, whatever, being intrinsic to such things, is the cause of their being, as the soul is the substance and cause of being of an animal. Third, the parts into which Platonists and Pythagoreans held such substances were ultimately resolved, such as surfaces, lines, points, and above all number, which they mistakenly regarded as the substance of everything, since removing it seemed to leave nothing; Aquinas rejects this, since what is universally required for a thing to exist need not itself be that thing's substance. Fourth, the quiddity or essence expressed by a thing's definition, which differs from form taken as a mere part, since essence includes the whole of what makes a thing what it is. Aquinas reduces these four to two fundamental senses: substance as the ultimate subject that is never itself predicated of anything else, a particular 'this something' existing separately and per se, and substance as the form or essence, that whereby a thing is what it is.",
  "B5C11": "Aquinas here treats what is 'the same,' beginning with sameness by accident, as when 'pale' and 'musical' are called the same only because both happen to belong to one subject, or when a subject and its accident are called the same because one is predicated of the other, as 'Socrates' and 'musical Socrates' are loosely called the same though never universally, since accidents belong to individuals and not to natures universally. Sameness that holds by a thing's own nature, he explains, follows the very senses already given for essential unity, whether grounded in one matter or in one substance through continuity or through an indivisible, unified account. From this Aquinas draws an important conclusion: sameness is a kind of oneness, either of several things found to converge in some single respect, or of one thing considered as though it were more than one, as when something is said to be the same as itself, which requires the intellect to treat what is really one as two termini in order to conceive a relation at all; such self-identity is therefore a relation of reason rather than a real relation, since a real relation would generate an infinite regress of further relations relating the thing to its own sameness. Things are called 'other' when their kinds, matters, or essential definitions are multiple, the direct opposite of sameness in every mode.",
  "B5C12": "Aquinas explains that 'different' properly applies only to things that, while agreeing in some respect, whether numerically, specifically, generically, or by proportion, nevertheless diverge in another; strictly, only things sharing some common ground are called different, since things sharing nothing at all differ, as it were, entirely through themselves rather than through anything else. Most properly different are things sharing the same genus yet divided by contrary differentiae, or things whose species are themselves contraries. Turning to 'like,' Aquinas presents three grades: things undergoing literally the same modification, things merely undergoing some modification each, whether the same or different, and, most properly, things sharing a single quality outright, such as two white objects. Something counts as more fully like another the more of the contrarieties relevant to alteration it shares, and especially when the shared quality is that thing's own proper quality rather than one merely held in common more loosely, as air resembles fire chiefly through heat, its own proper quality, rather than through some lesser point of overlap. Aquinas closes by noting that 'unlike' is simply the opposite of 'like' throughout, following the same distinctions in reverse, since wherever quality fails to converge in the relevant respects, dissimilarity rather than likeness results.",
  "B5C13": "Aquinas distinguishes four kinds of opposition: contradiction, contrariety, privation and possession, and relative terms, explaining that things are opposed either through dependency, which grounds relative opposition, or through some manner of removal, which yields three further grades depending on how much removal leaves behind. Sheer negation removes everything, leaving nothing; privation removes an attribute while leaving the subject that could have possessed it; and contrariety removes while leaving both subject and genus intact, since true contraries must share a common genus and often a common recipient subject. Aquinas shows that opposition can also be recognized through comparison to motion, since the terms from which and to which a change proceeds are opposed, as generation of the pale proceeds from the not-pale, and through comparison to a subject, since attributes that cannot both belong to one and the same receptive thing at once must be opposed, either themselves or the things underlying them. Contraries proper are things within one genus that differ maximally, whether absolutely, within a genus, or within a species, and secondary contraries are named by their relation to these, whether by possessing, receiving, producing, or losing genuine contraries. Finally, Aquinas lays out five ways things are called different in species: belonging to one genus without subordination, dividing one genus by a differentia, having subjects marked by contrary differentiae, being distinct ultimate species of one genus, or being contrary accidents unable to coexist in one subject.",
  "B5C14": "Aquinas shows that 'prior' and 'posterior' always presuppose some determinate beginning or principle, nearness to which makes a thing prior, whether that principle is fixed absolutely by nature or only relative to some further consideration, place, or particular people. He works through several orders in which this nearness is measured: place, where nearness to a naturally determined center or extreme, such as the world's middle or the heavens, makes a thing prior; time, where past events are prior the farther they lie from the present, but future events are prior the nearer they lie to it; motion, where nearness to the first mover makes a thing prior, so a child is prior to a grown man as nearer to the generative source, and this order extends to voluntary affairs, where the more powerful, whose will others must follow, counts as prior; and arrangement, as in a chorus or the strings of a lyre, where nearness to some fixed reference point determines priority. Aquinas then distinguishes priority in cognition, where universals are prior according to reason though individuals are prior to sense perception, and priority in nature and substance, which Plato used, whereby whatever can exist without other things while those others cannot exist without it is prior; this final sense, Aquinas notes, is ultimately what all the other modes of priority are reduced to, since what is prior never depends on what is posterior, whether the independence concerns generation or dissolution.",
  "B5C15": "Aquinas distinguishes active from passive potency as the two fundamental senses of power: active potency is a source of motion or change located in one thing insofar as it produces change in another, or in the same thing considered as other than itself, as the art of building resides in the builder rather than in the building; passive potency is, correspondingly, a source enabling a thing to be moved or affected by another. He notes a further refinement, that potency sometimes names merely the capacity to undergo or produce some effect at all, and sometimes more strictly the capacity to do so well or as intended, so that one who walks or speaks poorly may be said, in this stricter sense, not truly to be able to walk or speak. Aquinas also distinguishes states of firm resistance to corruption or alteration as a kind of potency, since durability against destructive forces reflects a positive strength rather than a mere absence of weakness. He then treats 'possible' and 'impossible' as used without reference to any real potency at all, but according to logical necessity: the impossible is whatever has a contrary that is necessarily true, as with an incommensurable diagonal, while the possible is whatever has no contrary that is necessarily false. Aquinas concludes that every derivative sense of potency and possibility ultimately refers back to this primary active potency, a genuine source of change in another thing, or in the same thing considered as other.",
  "B5C16": "Aquinas explains that quantity is whatever admits division into constituent parts, each of which is naturally capable of being one and a determinate 'this,' distinguishing this properly quantitative division from the resolution of a mixed body into elements present only virtually rather than actually within it. Quantity divides first into plurality, divisible into discontinuous parts and hence numerable, and magnitude, divisible into continuous parts and hence measurable, itself further specified as length, breadth, or depth according to whether continuity holds in one, two, or three dimensions, with number, line, surface, and solid arising as the limited or determinate versions of these. Aquinas then distinguishes quantity that belongs to a thing per se as its very substance, as a line is essentially a quantity, from quantity understood as an attribute or affection of such substantial quantities, as much and little, long and short, broad and narrow are affections respectively of number, line, and surface. He notes that great and small, and their comparative forms, belong to quantity by nature but are also transferred to other things. Finally he distinguishes two ways something is quantitative merely by accident: because it is an attribute of a quantified subject, as the musical is a quantum only because it belongs to a subject that is quantified, or because it is divisible only insofar as something else to which it is intrinsically tied, such as the magnitude traversed, is divisible, as motion and time are called quantities only in this derivative, posterior way.",
  "B5C17": "Aquinas distinguishes four senses of quality. The primary and proper sense is the differentia of a thing's essence, that by which one answers what sort a thing is within its genus, as man is qualified as two-footed and the circle as having no angles, so that the very differentia constituting a substance's kind is itself called a quality. A second sense applies to the unchanging objects of mathematics, as numbers are called qualified insofar as they are composite or incomposite, or resemble plane or solid figures through repeated multiplication, a quality that belongs to their essence beyond bare quantity. Third, quality names the changeable affections of movable substances, such as heat, cold, whiteness, and blackness, in respect of which bodies are said to alter, corresponding to the sensible qualities through which alteration properly occurs. Fourth, quality names virtue and vice, and more broadly good and evil, especially as found in living, purposive beings, since virtue and vice mark differences in how a thing acts or is acted upon well or badly. Aquinas reduces these four to two principal senses, the differentia of essence being primary, with mathematical quality falling under it as a kind of essential differentia, while the affections of things in motion form the second principal sense, under which virtue and vice fall as differentiae specifying activity as good or defective, a distinction found most fully in beings capable of deliberate choice.",
  "B5C18": "Aquinas distinguishes three basic modes of relation. The first concerns number, as double relates to half and multiple to submultiple, whether the numerical relation to unity or to another number is definite, like the sesquialter ratio of three to two, or indefinite, like the merely exceeding relation of a greater quantity to a lesser. The second concerns active and passive potency, as the heating relates to the heatable, and more generally every agent to what it acts upon. The third concerns measure and the measurable, as the knowable relates to knowledge and the perceptible to perception, a relation that, unlike the first two, does not hold reciprocally, since only the measurable is related to its measure and not conversely, because truth is measured by the reality known rather than the reverse. Aquinas explains the rationale behind this threefold division: one thing can be ordered to another according to being, which yields the third mode, according to active and passive power, which yields the second, or according to quantity capable of measurement, which yields the first. He also notes that equal, like, and same are relative according to unity taken absolutely rather than through comparison of numbers. Finally, some things are called relative only secondarily, either because their genus is relative, as medicine is relative because science is, because an abstract term is relative since its corresponding concrete term is, as equality is relative because the equal is, or merely by accident, as a man happens to be double something.",
  "B5C19": "Aquinas presents three senses of the complete. First, that outside which no part belonging to it can be found, as a complete span of time is one lacking no portion proper to it. Second, that which, in respect of excellence within its own kind, cannot be surpassed and lacks nothing belonging to its proper virtue, as a complete physician or flute player lacks nothing pertaining to skill in that craft; Aquinas notes this sense is even transferred, by an ironic extension, to bad things, so that a thoroughgoing thief or slanderer is called 'perfect' at being what he is, since virtue in general is what makes a thing good and its activity good, and the transferred usage mimics this structure even where the activity itself is vicious. Third, whatever has attained its end is called complete, since an end is something ultimate, and this too is stretched, by resemblance rather than truth, to things utterly ruined or destroyed, and even to death, called an end only because it is a terminus, not because it is genuinely that for the sake of which anything occurs. Aquinas observes that only the first principle, God, is complete absolutely and without qualification, lacking nothing in any respect and needing nothing further, while other things are called complete only relative to their own genus, as a man is called complete upon attaining happiness.",
  "B5C20": "Aquinas identifies four senses of limit. First, the outermost point of a thing, beyond which none of its own parts can be found and within which every part of it lies, as a point limits a line and a surface limits a body. Second, one extreme of a motion or action, specifically the terminus toward which the change tends rather than that from which it proceeds, though sometimes both extremes are loosely called limits, since every motion lies between two termini. Third, that for the sake of which something occurs, the ultimate object of an intention, corresponding to the terminus of motion in the practical order. Fourth, the substance or essence of a thing, expressed by its definition, since a definition is the terminus of cognition, the point beyond which nothing further need be sought to know what a thing is, and, because knowledge proceeds by the knower's assimilation to the thing known, what limits knowledge of a thing in this way must also, in some sense, limit the thing itself. Aquinas concludes by comparing limit to beginning: limit has as many senses as beginning and more, since every beginning is itself a kind of limit, but not every limit counts as a beginning, for the terminus toward which a motion tends is a limit yet in no way its starting principle.",
  "B5C21": "Aquinas treats together 'that in virtue of which' and 'in virtue of itself.' The former has several senses corresponding to the senses of cause: the form or substance of a thing, as the good itself is that in virtue of which a man is good; the proximate subject in which an attribute naturally resides, as color exists in virtue of a surface; and, more loosely, the end for which, or the reason why, something occurs. 'In virtue of itself' likewise divides into several senses. First, the very essence of a thing, since Callias is, in virtue of himself, Callias and whatever it was for him to be Callias. Second, whatever is included in a thing's definition, as Callias is in virtue of himself an animal, since animal belongs to what he essentially is. Third, whatever a subject receives directly, in itself or in one of its parts, as a surface is white in virtue of itself, or a man is alive in virtue of himself because the soul, in which life directly resides, is a part of him. Fourth, whatever has no cause beyond itself, as man, despite having several partial causes such as animality and two-footedness, is nonetheless man in virtue of himself without further explanation. Fifth, whatever belongs to a thing considered strictly alone and apart from everything else, as when something is said to exist 'in virtue of itself' meaning simply by itself, in isolation.",
  "B5C22": "Aquinas explains that disposition names the arrangement of the parts of something that has parts, whether that arrangement concerns place, potency, or kind, and observes that some determinate positioning or ordering is implied even by the word itself. He distinguishes three modes corresponding to the different ways parts can be ordered: an ordering according to place, which falls under the category of position or situs; an ordering according to power or capacity, whereby a thing is disposed well or badly, as through health or sickness, which falls under the first species of quality; and an ordering according to the specific form or shape of the whole, which falls under quantity, since some quantities, like lines, surfaces, bodies, and place, admit position among their parts, while others, like number and time, do not. Because this brief chapter treats a term of relatively narrow philosophical weight compared to the great terms surrounding it, such as unity, being, and substance, Aquinas moves through it economically, emphasizing chiefly that disposition always signifies some kind of order among parts, whatever the respect in which that order is considered, rather than developing an extended set of further distinctions, since the underlying notion, unlike quantity or quality themselves, does not constitute one of the primary categories but only a modification found within them.",
  "B5C23": "Aquinas explains that 'having' or 'holding' bears several senses. First, to treat a thing according to one's own nature or impulse, as fever is said to have a man, tyrants to have their cities, and a person to have the clothes he wears, since in each case the haver directs or governs what is had. Second, that in which something exists as in a receptive subject is said to have it, as bronze has the shape of a statue and a body has a disease, this sense embracing the having of any accident, quality, or form whatsoever. Third, whatever contains is said to have what it contains, as a vessel has the liquid within it, a city its inhabitants, and a ship its sailors, and in this sense a whole is said to have its parts, since it contains them much as a place contains what occupies it, though a whole, unlike a place, is not divided from its parts as a place is divided from what it holds. Fourth, whatever prevents a thing from moving or acting according to its own natural impulse is said to have it, as pillars are said to have the weights pressing upon them, and, in poetic language, Atlas is said to have the heavens, a myth Aquinas traces back to an astronomer who studied the heavenly motions closely. Aquinas notes finally that 'being in something' parallels 'having' point for point.",
  "B5C24": "Aquinas distinguishes several senses in which one thing is said to come from another. First, and most properly, from matter, whether the most general kind, as everything meltable is said to come from water broadly considered, or the specific and proximate matter, as a statue comes from bronze in particular. Second, from the first moving principle, as a fight comes from an insult that provoked it. Third, from the composite of matter and form, taken as a whole, as parts come from a whole and verses come from an entire poem, this sense working in the direction of resolution rather than composition, since form is the end toward which generation moves, and what is complete is precisely what has attained its form. Fourth, as a species comes from a part of the specific formula, as man comes from 'two-footed' and a syllable from a letter, which Aquinas distinguishes from the material sense because here the composition concerns a part of the intelligible form itself rather than sensible matter. He notes a fifth, looser use whereby something is said to come from another because it comes from some part of that other, as a child comes from father and mother, and finally a sixth sense involving mere succession in time without any real transformation, as night is said to come from day, or a voyage from the equinox, simply because one follows the other.",
  "B5C25": "Aquinas distinguishes several senses of 'part.' In the broadest sense, whatever a quantity can in any way be divided into counts as a part, so that two is, loosely speaking, a part of three; more strictly, only that which measures the whole exactly is called its part, so that two is not, in this stricter sense, a part of three, though it is a part of four, since twice two makes four. Beyond quantity, the elements into which a kind divides, apart from any consideration of quantity, are also called parts, so that the species are said to be parts of their genus. Further, the constituents into which a whole, understood either as a form or as the concrete thing possessing that form, is divided or of which it is composed count as parts, as both the bronze, serving as matter, and the characteristic angle, belonging to the form, are parts of a bronze cube. Finally, the constituents included within the definition explaining what a thing is are also called its parts, which is why genus can be called a part of species in this sense, even though, taken in the second sense above, species is rather said to be a part of genus. Aquinas notes that the third sense concerns parts of the concrete thing itself, whether of the species or of the individual, while the fourth concerns parts belonging strictly to the intelligible formula.",
  "B5C26": "Aquinas explains that a whole is, first, that from which none of the parts naturally belonging to it is missing, and, second, that which so contains what it contains that these form a genuine unity, which happens in two ways. In one way, as a universal whole, where each of the things contained is itself, individually, the very thing predicated as whole, as man, horse, and any other animal are each simply an animal, so that the universal is predicated of each part taken separately. In the other way, as a continuous and limited whole, a genuine unity composed of several parts, more fully a whole when those parts exist in it potentially, as in a continuous magnitude, than when they exist there only actually side by side, as stones piled in a heap, and more fully a whole when the unity is natural rather than produced merely by art. Aquinas notes a further distinction bearing on how position affects such wholes: things like water or wine, whose parts can be rearranged without altering the thing at all, are properly called 'totals' and spoken of with 'all' rather than 'whole'; things like a man or a house, whose determinate arrangement of parts is essential to what they are, are properly called wholes; and things like wax or a garment, which retain their underlying material through rearrangement even while losing their particular shape, admit both descriptions at once.",
  "B5C27": "Aquinas lays out the conditions something must meet to be properly called mutilated or maimed. It must first be a quantitative whole capable of division into parts, since a universal whole cannot be mutilated merely by the loss of one of its species. It must further be divisible into unlike, distinguishable parts, so that homogeneous wholes like water or fire, whose parts do not differ from one another, cannot properly be mutilated, and it must possess a determinate position among those parts, which is why a musical harmony, though built of unlike high and low notes, still cannot be called mutilated, since removing one note does not leave a maimed harmony behind but simply a different arrangement. Two-part things cannot be mutilated by losing one part, since what is removed is never merely equal to what remains; and no number can be mutilated at all, since removing or adding even a single unit changes what kind of number results, whereas mutilation requires that the thing's substance survive the loss, as a cup remains recognizably a cup even after part of it is broken away. Finally, Aquinas specifies that the part lost must be an extremity rather than a central portion, and one that does not naturally grow back, which is why losing a hand or foot counts as mutilation while losing hair, which regenerates, or a spleen, which lies centrally, does not.",
  "B5C28": "Aquinas distinguishes four senses of genus or race. First, the continuous generation of things sharing one and the same species, as one speaks of the human race continuing so long as generation among men persists. Second, that from which things first proceed as from their begetter, as the Hellenes are named for their descent from Hellen, a sense drawn more often from the father than the mother, though sometimes, as with the Pleiades, from a female ancestor. Third, genus functions as the common subject underlying specifically different accidents, as surface is genus to plane figures and solid to solid figures, this sense resembling matter, since the proper subject is placed in the definition of an accident much as genus is placed in the definition of a species. Fourth, and most properly for definitions, genus names the first element placed in a definition, predicated in answer to 'what is it,' of which the differentiae function as further qualifications, as animal stands to man. Aquinas reduces the third and fourth senses to a single underlying idea, genus functioning after the manner of matter to the differentia's manner of form. He then distinguishes two ways things are 'other in genus': through having irreducibly different proximate subjects, which concerns the natural philosopher, or through falling under different categories of predication altogether, which concerns the logician, since the categories neither reduce into one another nor into some further common genus.",
  "B5C29": "Aquinas distinguishes the false first as it concerns things themselves, in two ways: either because a proposition about a matter simply cannot, or in some case does not, hold true, as an impossible falsehood like a commensurable diagonal is always false while a merely contingent falsehood, like someone sitting who in fact is standing, is false only sometimes; or because something real is naturally apt to appear other than it is or to appear as something that does not exist at all, as a shadow-picture or a dream produces a likeness without being the reality it resembles. Turning to false accounts, Aquinas explains that a definition is false, strictly, only when applied to something other than what it truly defines, since the very same definition true of one thing is false of another, as the definition of a circle is false when applied to a triangle; he uses this to refute Antisthenes, who denied that falsehood or even contradiction was possible on the mistaken assumption that only a thing's own proper definition could ever be predicated of it, whereas something can, in a qualified way, truly bear an account belonging properly to something else. Finally, a man is called false who is disposed toward, and delights in, producing false accounts for their own sake, which is why the argument in the Hippias confusing mere capacity to deceive with being habitually false is mistaken, since even the prudent man can deceive without thereby being a false man.",
  "B5C30": "Aquinas distinguishes two senses of accident. The first names whatever truly belongs to a subject yet neither necessarily nor even usually, but only in the fewest cases, having no determinate or per se cause but only some incidental, chance cause, as a man digging a hole to plant something happens upon buried treasure, or a musician happens also to be pale; neither outcome follows from what the man was actually doing, and Aquinas notes there is no necessary connection or even any consistent frequency binding the two. Similarly, a voyage that lands unintentionally at Aegina, driven there by a storm rather than by design, illustrates accidental causation, since the sailor's arrival follows not from his purpose in sailing but from an entirely extraneous cause. The second sense names whatever belongs to a subject per se, in virtue of what that subject is, yet still does not belong to its essence or substance, as a triangle's having angles equal to two right angles belongs to it necessarily and always, yet does not enter into the definition of what a triangle is. Aquinas notes the crucial difference between the two senses: accidents of the first kind can never be perpetual, since by nature they occur only rarely, while accidents of the second kind can indeed be eternal, following with strict necessity from the thing's nature even though they remain, properly speaking, outside its essence.",
  "B6C1": "Aquinas reads this opening chapter as Aristotle's attempt to mark off metaphysics from every other branch of knowledge by showing what is distinctive about its subject matter. Every science, he notes, goes searching for causes and starting points, but each of the particular sciences carves off some limited region of reality - a genus - and studies that alone, taking for granted that its subject exists and simply working out its properties. None of them asks the deeper question of what it means for something to be at all. Metaphysics alone takes being itself, considered simply as being, for its object, and so alone inquires into the causes and principles of everything without restriction. To make this vivid, Aquinas walks through the three theoretical sciences that Aristotle distinguishes according to their relation to matter and motion. Natural philosophy studies substances that involve both matter and change, since its objects cannot even be defined without reference to a movable subject. Mathematics studies things that are abstracted from sensible matter by the mind's own act of separation, even though such things do not actually exist apart from bodies. The highest of the three, which Aristotle calls theology and which Aquinas identifies with first philosophy, considers substance that is both immaterial and immovable in its very existence, not merely as conceived by abstraction. Because this third science treats the highest and most universal causes - causes on which the being of everything else depends - it deserves the name of first philosophy, and it is this science, Aquinas insists, that alone is equipped to study being as being.",
  "B6C2": "Here Aquinas explains why Aristotle excludes accidental being from the concerns of metaphysics even though metaphysics studies being universally. He first lays out the different senses in which something can be said to be: being by accident, being as truth (a matter of how the mind joins or separates concepts), being according to the ten categories, and being as divided into potency and act. Having distinguished these, Aristotle sets aside the first two so that the science can concentrate on being under the categories, understood through potency and act. Aquinas's explanation of why accidental being escapes scientific treatment turns on the nature of science itself: genuine knowledge grasps what holds always or for the most part, tracing an effect back to a per se cause that reliably produces it. An accidental combination, such as a builder who happens also to be a physician and so happens to restore someone's health, involves no such regular connection; the joining of the two facts is a matter of coincidence rather than of one thing's nature producing another. Because such combinations have no determinate, per se cause, they cannot be foreseen, taught, or demonstrated, and so no art or science can be devoted to them. Aquinas connects this observation to the sophists, who trade precisely on confusing what belongs to a subject accidentally with what belongs to it essentially, generating deceptive arguments from that ambiguity. He also notes, importantly, that acknowledging accidental being does not commit one to denying necessity altogether in the world; it simply recognizes that alongside what happens always or usually, there remains a residue of things that merely happen to coincide, and these fall outside the reach of demonstrative knowledge.",
  "B6C3": "In this chapter Aquinas presents Aristotle as answering a determinist who would deny that anything happens by chance at all, on the ground that every event follows of necessity from some prior cause, which in turn follows from an earlier cause, and so on back to causes already present or past - and what is already present or past cannot fail to have its effect. Aristotle's reply, as Aquinas unpacks it, is that this reasoning proves too much: if it were sound, absolutely everything that will ever happen would already be necessary now, which plainly conflicts with the evident fact that many things could go one way or another. The flaw lies in assuming that every causal chain must be unbroken and per se; in reality such chains regularly terminate in a cause that produces its effect only accidentally, not by its own proper power, and at that point the chain of necessity simply stops, leaving room for the event to have gone otherwise. Aquinas takes the occasion to relate this to broader questions about fate and divine providence, a topic he treats with some care. He explains that lower, particular causes can fail or be impeded by contrary circumstances, which is precisely why their effects are only usual rather than strictly necessary, and why free rational choice is not bound by the sweep of the heavenly bodies acting on bodily nature. Yet none of this contingency escapes the ordering of divine providence, which, precisely because it operates on an entirely higher level than any particular cause, can hold all things - including contingent and accidental happenings - within a single order without thereby forcing them to happen of necessity.",
  "B6C4": "Having dismissed accidental being, Aquinas explains that Aristotle now turns to a second sense of being that likewise falls outside the proper scope of metaphysics: being as truth, and its opposite, non-being as falsity. His central claim, as Aquinas draws it out, is that truth and falsity are not features of things in the world taken by themselves, but belong strictly to the operation of the intellect. A thing simply is or is not what it is; it becomes true or false only in relation to a mind that either correctly or incorrectly represents it. More precisely, truth and falsity arise only where the intellect composes or divides - that is, where it affirms that some subject and predicate belong together or denies that they do. When the mind joins what is in fact united in reality, or separates what is in fact distinct, its judgment is true; when it does the reverse, the judgment is false. Aquinas stresses that this composing and dividing must be understood as an instantaneous act of the intellect grasping a single unified content, not a temporal succession of separate thoughts. He also notes that with regard to simple, uncomposed apprehension of what a thing is, there is no truth or falsity at all, since error only enters once the mind ventures to affirm or deny something of a subject. Because this sense of being tracks something added by the mind's own activity rather than the constitution of things themselves, it, like accidental being, must be set aside from the inquiry into being as being, clearing the ground for Aristotle to take up the causes and principles of substance properly speaking.",
  "B7C1": "Aquinas reads this opening chapter as the hinge of the whole treatise: having already set aside accidental being and being-as-truth from the science's main business, Aristotle now turns to being-in-itself, and Aquinas shows that this science really reduces to the study of substance. Among the ten categories, substance alone is what \"being\" means without qualification. When we ask what quality or size something has, we answer with color or measurement; but when we ask what a thing is, we answer with a substance-word, man or god, never with white or three-cubits-long. Every other category is called \"being\" only derivatively, because it belongs to some substance. Aquinas draws out three respects in which substance is first: in time, since no accident can exist without some substance, though a substance can exist stripped of any particular accident; in definition, because an accident's account must include its subject, the way \"snub\" includes \"nose\"; and in knowledge, because we say we truly know something only when we grasp what it is, not merely its quantity or location. Aquinas then notes how Aristotle ties this to the history of philosophy: the ancient and perennial question \"what is being?\" has always really been the question \"what is substance?\"—those who argued for one principle or many, finite or infinite, were all disputing about substances without knowing it. This is why the inquiry of the entire book must concentrate almost exclusively on substance.",
  "B7C2": "Aquinas treats this chapter as a survey of received opinion, gathered before any rigorous determination is attempted, so that the inquiry can start from what is least controversial. Bodies are the most obvious substances: animals, plants, the elements, and things composed from them, up through the heavenly bodies. But thinkers disagree once one goes further. Some held that the boundaries of a body—surface, line, point, unit—are more truly substance than the body itself, on the assumption that what is simpler and prior is more real. Plato went further still, positing two orders of separate substance beyond sensible bodies: the Forms and the objects of mathematics, added to sensible substance as a third kind. Speusippus multiplied kinds of substance even more, deriving from a first \"One\" separate principles for number, for magnitude, and for soul, each functioning as its own independent order of substance. Still others collapsed Plato's distinction between Forms and numbers, treating them as one nature, from which lines and surfaces and eventually the heavens and sensible bodies proceed in a descending series. Aquinas notes that Aristotle withholds judgment on all of this for now, promising later to determine which claims are sound and whether anything exists apart from sensible substance. But he insists that any such judgment presupposes first sketching what sensible substance itself is, since this is the one point everyone concedes, and the natural starting place for the inquiry that follows.",
  "B7C3": "Aquinas identifies four things commonly called substance: essence, universal, genus, and subject or substratum, and he explains why Aristotle begins with the subject, since what most literally underlies everything else seems substance in the fullest sense. This subject itself divides three ways: matter, form, and the composite of both, related not as species under a genus but analogically, by priority and posteriority. Aquinas walks through the reasoning that form outranks matter, since matter is mere potency while form is act, and act is naturally and even temporally prior to potency; and since form outranks matter, it likewise outranks the composite, which depends on both. Yet a serious complication arises: strip away every quantity, quality, and accident from a sensible body, and matter alone seems to remain, tempting the conclusion that matter alone is truly substance. Aquinas explains that the ancients who reasoned this way were misled by their ignorance of substantial form, having no concept beyond the sensible accidents; he shows why the conclusion fails regardless, since substance properly requires two marks, separability and being a determinate \"this,\" and matter possesses neither on its own, since of itself it is pure potency, becoming actual and determinate only through form. Form, and the composite it informs, are therefore the better candidates for substance. Aquinas notes that Aristotle sets the composite aside for now as evidently posterior and knowable enough, and turns to the far more perplexing question of form, proceeding deliberately from what is better known to us toward what is better known by nature.",
  "B7C4": "Here Aquinas follows Aristotle's investigation of essence, what a thing is said to be strictly through itself and not accidentally. Being white is not the essence of \"surface,\" since white and surface have different accounts; nor is \"white surface\" the essence of surface, since the term surface would then reappear redundantly within its own definition. A genuine statement of essence expresses a thing's meaning without repeating the term itself and without smuggling in anything foreign to it. This raises the question whether things named by the other categories, or accidental compounds like \"white man\" or the example of \"cloak,\" have essences and definitions in the same sense substances do. Aquinas lays out the resolution taking shape: strictly and primarily, essence and definition belong only to substance, because only there does the answer to \"what is it\" name something self-subsistent; but in an extended, secondary sense, an accident can be said to have an essence insofar as its account is referred back to some substance, much as \"medicinal\" is said of a patient's body, a treatment, and an instrument, not univocally, and not by mere chance equivocation, but by relation to one thing, the art of medicine. Aquinas stresses that this is the same pattern by which \"being\" itself applies primarily to substance and only derivatively, through a kind of proportional likeness, to quantity, quality, and the rest—so essence, like being, has a primary home in substance and a borrowed, dependent sense everywhere else.",
  "B7C5": "Aquinas takes up the harder case of compound terms such as \"snub,\" which cannot be defined without naming their subject. Concavity can be defined without mentioning nose, but snubness cannot, since snubness simply is concavity found specifically in a nose—nose belongs to its very account, not as an accidental addition the way whiteness attaches to Callias. This produces an apparent dilemma: if \"snub nose\" means nothing but \"concave nose,\" then to define snub nose would be to say \"nose\" twice over, since concave already implies nose, threatening either absurdity or an infinite regress of repeated terms. Aquinas explains how Aristotle resolves this without abandoning the deeper point already reached: essence and definition belong properly and without qualification to substance alone; but in a secondary, dependent way they can be granted to composite accidents like snubness, provided one recognizes that such an attribute's very account requires its subject, whether that subject appears openly, as in the concrete term \"snub,\" or is folded in, as in the abstract term \"snubness,\" rightly defined as concavity of a nose. Aquinas draws the wider lesson: attributes that inhere in a determinate subject by their own nature, and not merely by accident, cannot be understood or even named apart from that subject, unlike a purely accidental union such as \"white man.\" The upshot, which Aquinas takes as settled for the rest of the book, is that essence and definition in the full and primary sense belong to substances alone, and to everything else only in a diminished, borrowed way.",
  "B7C6": "Aquinas presents Aristotle's inquiry into whether a thing is identical with its own essence. In things said strictly through themselves—substances existing in their own right—thing and essence must be the same, not by accident but according to their very account. Aquinas reconstructs the argument: were a thing's essence something other than the thing, that essence would itself require a further essence, and so on without end, and knowledge itself would become impossible, since to know a thing just is to know its essence; sever the two and neither remains either knowable or, in a sense, real. Aquinas is careful to note that this reasoning is aimed directly at Plato's separately existing Forms: sensible substances need no separate Idea standing behind them to secure their being or their intelligibility, since each already possesses its essence intrinsically and identically with itself. Merely accidental unities, by contrast, like \"white man,\" are not simply identical with their essence, since such a composite includes something, whiteness, foreign to the subject's own nature. Aquinas adds an important refinement often overlooked: even within genuine substances, the abstract essence-term is not wholly the same as the concrete thing—\"humanity\" signifies only the essential principles, excluding every accident, while \"man\" signifies the whole subject, in which accidents also inhere; humanity signifies as a part, man as the whole. Only where no accidents exist at all, as in God, do the abstract and the concrete coincide without remainder.",
  "B7C7": "Aquinas walks through Aristotle's threefold division of coming-to-be—by nature, by art, and by chance or spontaneity—and the three elements every generation requires: an agent, a matter from which (the underlying subject, not the mere privation), and a product, which may fall under any category though it belongs most properly to substance. In natural generation, matter, the operative form present in the begetting agent, and the specific form actually produced in the offspring are all called \"nature,\" though in different senses: matter as the underlying substrate, the engendered form as the term toward which the process moves, and the parent's own form as the active principle, specifically alike to what it produces, though numerically distinct, since man begets man but not the very same man. Aquinas highlights Aristotle's analogy from medicine: the physician reasons backward from the form of health he intends, through a chain of intermediate steps, until reaching some immediate action he can himself perform, such as inducing warmth by rubbing; production is simply this same reasoning reversed, moving forward from that starting action to the intended form. Aquinas underscores that everything produced, by nature or art alike, requires matter genuinely capable both of being and of not-being. He also notes the precise way things are named from what they come from: a healed man is called simply \"man,\" not \"the sick one,\" and a statue is called \"golden\" rather than \"gold,\" because coming-to-be involves the real transformation of a substrate, not the mere persistence of unchanged material with a shape added.",
  "B7C8": "Aquinas treats this chapter as delivering Aristotle's decisive blow against Plato's separated Forms as causes of generation. Since production requires matter, agent, and product, the agent does not, strictly speaking, make the matter, which already pre-exists, nor does it, strictly, make the form: when a bronze sphere is produced, the sphere-shape is said to \"come to be\" only incidentally, because what is truly generated is the composite, this bronze now made spherical. Aquinas reconstructs the proof: were the agent required to make the form as such, that form would itself need to arise from some further matter, making the form itself composite of matter and form, and generating an endless regress. Hence only the composite of form and matter is strictly generated; the form or essence is not brought into being at all but is \"drawn out\" from matter's own potency. From this, Aquinas explains, Aristotle concludes that no separately existing universal sphere, house, or man need be posited to explain generation, since the begetter is always itself a concrete individual producing another concrete individual like itself in kind—man begets man, and even the anomalous case of a mule shows the seed's inherent formative power transmitting the nearest attainable likeness. Aquinas stresses that this removes the Platonic need for a Form serving either as an active generating cause or as a separate exemplar-pattern to be imitated, since the individual agent's own form, already present in matter, is entirely sufficient to explain both the production and the specific likeness of what is produced.",
  "B7C9": "Aquinas explains why some things, like health, can arise either through art or spontaneously by chance, while others, like a house, arise only through art. The difference lies in the matter: some matter contains within itself a principle able to move itself toward the relevant form, as the human body possesses an inherent capacity for healing, so that health can sometimes occur \"by chance,\" through mere incidental warming, without any physician's intention; other matter, such as stone and timber, has no such internal motive principle and can be brought to its form only by an external agent possessing the relevant skill. Aquinas notes the parallel Aristotle draws with natural generation: seed carries the form potentially and operates much like an artisan's skill, which is why some living things reproducible from seed can, on ancient biological assumptions, also arise spontaneously from suitably disposed matter, while others require the parent animal itself. Aquinas draws out the chapter's central conclusion, comparing it to a syllogism: just as every syllogism ultimately proceeds from and is resolved into some account of what a thing is, so every process of production ultimately starts from and is explained by substance, that is, by the essential form toward which the process tends. He adds that this same pattern, proved first for substance, extends analogously to quality and quantity, though with an important asymmetry: substantial generation strictly requires that another actual substance already exist to produce it, whereas a quality or quantity need only pre-exist potentially in the substance undergoing change.",
  "B7C10": "Aquinas treats this long chapter as resolving whether the parts into which a definition analyzes must themselves belong to the definition of the whole. He notes the puzzle's two pulling examples: a circle's definition omits any definition of its segments, yet a syllable's definition includes the definition of its letters, though a circle divides into segments just as a syllable divides into letters. A related difficulty concerns priority: parts like the acute angle within a right angle, or a finger within an animal, seem prior to their wholes, since the whole is explained through them, yet a finger cannot exist, except equivocally, once severed from a living body, suggesting the whole is prior instead. Aquinas explains Aristotle's resolution through a crucial distinction of senses of \"part.\" Some parts belong to a thing's matter, such as the bronze constituting a statue, or flesh and bone materially constituting a man; other parts belong properly to the form or intelligible account, such as the letters that constitute what it is to be a syllable. Only these latter, formal parts belong in a genuine definition, since definition concerns the universal species and not material individuation—this is why the material segments of this or that particular circle are excluded from the definition of circle-as-such, while letters, being constitutive of a syllable's very nature, are included. Aquinas illustrates the principle through the soul, which as form is prior to the body's organs; a severed finger, lacking the soul that made it functionally a finger, is a finger in name only.",
  "B7C11": "Aquinas presents Aristotle here closing a gap left open by the previous chapter: which parts belong to form, and so to definition, and which belong only to the concrete composite? Where a form clearly occurs in different kinds of matter, as a circle can be bronze, wood, or stone, it is obvious that this particular matter is no part of the circle's essence. But where a form is always found embedded in one determinate matter, as man is always found in flesh and bone, judgment is harder, and Aquinas notes that some thinkers wrongly concluded, on the model of the circle, that flesh and bone must equally be excluded from man's essence, reducing definitions ultimately to bare numbers, since stripping continuous extension from geometric figures leaves only units. Aquinas details Aristotle's refutation: this reduction absurdly collapses distinct kinds into one, since a triangle, a syllogism, and a body all \"share\" the number three without being the same kind of thing; and it is simply false that natural definitions can dispense with matter altogether. Unlike a circle, an animal is essentially something sensible, undefinable without reference to motion and therefore to properly disposed bodily parts—a hand counts as a hand only while alive and capable of its function—which is why the comparison some drew between animal and circle fails. Aquinas concludes that natural species genuinely include a kind of matter, called sensible matter, within their definition, and mathematical objects include an analogous intelligible matter, continuous extension, refuting any attempt to strip matter from either kind of definition.",
  "B7C12": "Aquinas explains the puzzle Aristotle takes up here: why is a definition built from genus and differentiae, such as \"two-footed animal,\" one single formula rather than a mere list of separate terms? Unlike a purely accidental unity such as \"pale man,\" where the two terms do not intrinsically belong together, genus and differentia must combine into a genuine unity, and this is puzzling especially since a genus can seemingly be divided by many differentiae in succession. Aquinas traces Aristotle's solution through the method of division: a correct definition results only when each differentia divides the one before it by a further differentia proper to that very respect—footed animal, for instance, correctly divided not into feathered and featherless, an accidental cut, but into cloven-footed and non-cloven, a division intrinsic to footedness itself. When division proceeds this way, every earlier and more generic differentia is effectively contained already within the last, most specific one, so that spelling out the whole chain in words is really only unpacking, redundantly, what the final differentia alone already says. The genuine definition, therefore, is constituted strictly by this last differentia, which turns out to be identical with the thing's very form and substance; nothing is added by repeating the earlier terms once it is reached. Aquinas notes that this is why reordering a properly constructed definition exposes redundancy at once, and why there is, properly speaking, no temporal or logical succession among a substance's differentiae, only a nested unity that culminates in one final, defining differentia.",
  "B7C13": "Aquinas turns to whether universals, as the Platonists held, can themselves be substances. He carefully distinguishes two senses of \"universal\": the common nature itself, such as animal or man, which does signify substance when predicated of what a thing is, and that same nature considered precisely as universal, that is, as one thing shared by many—and it is only in this second sense that Aristotle denies it is substance. Aquinas lays out the arguments in turn: a thing's substance is proper to it and belongs to nothing else, but a universal by definition is common to many, so it cannot be the substance of all of them together, since then all would collapse into one, nor of any single one alone, since it belongs equally to the rest. Substance, moreover, is what is never said of a subject, while a universal is always predicated of some subject. A further, more technical argument, which Aquinas explains with care, holds that no substance can be composed of substances actually present within it, since two things actually distinct are never thereby made into one actual thing—only what is merely potentially two can become actually one; here Aquinas credits Democritus with seeing the principle correctly, even though he wrongly concluded that magnitudes must therefore be indivisible. Since universals are not substances, and no substance is composed of already-actual substances, Aquinas notes that Aristotle leaves a real difficulty hanging: if no substance can be composite in either of these ways, none seems definable at all, a knot Aristotle only unties later, by showing that substance is composed of substances potentially, not actually.",
  "B7C14": "Aquinas shows how Aristotle presses the previous chapter's conclusion directly against Platonists who make Forms separately subsisting substances while also holding that each Form's definition is composed of genus and differentia. If \"animal\" is one and the same nature present in both a separately existing Man-itself and Horse-itself, Aquinas explains, then either it is numerically one and the same thing in both, which is impossible, since one undivided thing cannot exist wholly within several separately subsisting things at once, and would also have to bear contrary differentiae, such as two-footed and many-footed, simultaneously; or else it is different in each species, in which case there would be as many distinct \"animals\" as there are kinds, and each of those, in turn, would need its own participation in some prior Animal-itself, launching a vicious regress with no coherent account of how the levels relate. Aquinas notes that Aristotle presses the point further by comparing genus to individual sensible things rather than merely to species, since the gap the Platonists must bridge is even wider there, making the same absurdities still more severe. The chapter's conclusion, Aquinas stresses, is unqualified: there simply are no separately subsisting Forms of sensible things of the kind the Platonists proposed, because the theory cannot satisfy its own basic requirement—one single nature genuinely and coherently shared, without contradiction, across several distinct individuals.",
  "B7C15": "Aquinas explains Aristotle's demonstration that Platonic Ideas, precisely because they are conceived as separately subsisting individuals, cannot after all be defined—undermining the very purpose for which Plato introduced them, namely to secure fixed objects for definition and demonstration, which changeable sensible things cannot supply. First, individual sensible substances cannot properly be defined or demonstrated about, because their matter is genuinely capable of both being and not-being, making them corruptible; once they pass from the senses through which they are known, no account can reliably track them, whereas definition and demonstration require necessary, unchanging objects. Since a Platonic Idea, however eternal, is still conceived as an individual \"this,\" Aquinas shows it suffers an analogous defect: any definitional formula must be built from several terms, and each term, to be intelligible at all, must be common to more than one thing, since a definition using only names peculiar to a single individual explains nothing genuinely. But then whatever definition is assigned to one Idea will necessarily apply equally to whatever those constituent common terms name elsewhere, destroying the Idea's claimed uniqueness, and even implying, absurdly, that the constituent terms, such as \"animal\" and \"two-footed,\" are themselves prior to and separable from the supposedly primary Idea of man. Aquinas notes that Aristotle extends the same reasoning even to genuinely unique eternal individuals like the sun: defining the sun by accidental features such as circling the earth or being hidden at night is illegitimate, since the sun would remain the sun even if these features were removed.",
  "B7C16": "Aquinas explains this chapter's two main arguments. First, Aristotle shows that most things ordinarily called substances—the bodily parts of animals, and even earth, air, fire, and water once mixed into a compound—exist only potentially, not actually, so long as they remain genuine parts of a continuous, unified whole; only once physically separated does a former \"part\" become an actual, independent thing, and at that point it is no longer strictly a part at all. Aquinas notes that living organs come closest to actuality, since they possess an intrinsic principle of motion at their joints, which explains why certain divided animals briefly continue to live, but even these remain merely potential parts as long as the organism stays one continuous natural whole; union produced only by external force or grafting, by contrast, counts as a kind of violence against nature rather than genuine unity. Second, Aristotle argues that neither \"being\" nor \"unity\"—nor \"principle,\" \"element,\" or \"cause\"—can itself be the substance of anything, since substance belongs properly only to the individual thing possessing it, and what is truly one, unlike what is merely common, cannot exist simultaneously in many places at once. Aquinas closes by explaining Aristotle's balanced verdict on Plato: right in recognizing that if common natures were substances they would have to exist separately, since substance is by nature self-subsistent, but wrong in supposing one nature could be both separately subsistent and, at the same time, common to many things at once—an error Aquinas traces to the Platonists having no independent grasp of what immaterial substance actually is, so that they simply projected familiar sensible natures onto a separate, immaterial plane.",
  "B7C17": "Aquinas explains that Aristotle now approaches substance from a fresh starting point, as principle and cause, in order finally to say in reality, and not merely logically, what substance-as-essence is. The method: a genuine \"why\" question always seeks a cause connecting some predicate to a subject distinct from it—\"why does it thunder\" reduces to \"why is sound produced in the clouds\"—while asking why a thing is simply itself is empty, since it presupposes the very identity that would need explaining. Applied to a composite like a house, Aquinas shows, the real question is why these bricks and stones constitute a house at all, and the answer sought is precisely the essence or form, which functions sometimes as an end and sometimes as a first mover, but always as the cause by which matter becomes this determinate thing. Aquinas highlights Aristotle's central insight about composite wholes generally: a syllable is not simply identical with its letters, nor flesh with fire and earth, because something further, beyond the material elements themselves, accounts for their being unified into one thing rather than a mere heap; and this further principle cannot itself be reduced to another element without triggering an endless regress. Aquinas identifies this unifying principle as the thing's substantial form or nature, itself not composed of elements but rather the very cause that binds elements into one determinate substance. With this the book's inquiry closes by locating substance most properly in form, understood as intrinsic formal cause and principle of unity, preparing the way for Book Eight's continued examination of composite substances in light of these same principles.",
  "B8C1": "Aquinas reads this chapter as a hinge: Book VII treated substance logically, through definition and its parts, while Book VIII turns to apply that groundwork to the natural substances actually before us. He recalls the earlier inventory - the substances everyone grants (the elements, plants and animals, the heavens) alongside the disputed ones some schools add (Forms, mathematical objects), and the further complication that genus and the universal were once mistaken for substance because Ideas ride on that same claim. Book VII, he notes, already dismantled the notion that genus or universal could be substance, and settled what a definition is and what its parts are. With that logical work behind him, Aristotle now resumes the sensible substances directly. Every one of them, Aquinas stresses, involves matter, and he distinguishes three ways \"substance\" gets used here: matter is substance only potentially, as that which is not yet a determinate thing but is capable of becoming one; form or actuality is substance as that which can be conceived, though not physically separated, apart from matter; and the composite of the two is substance in the fullest sense, since it alone is generated, destroyed, and capable of independent existence - form and matter themselves are never strictly generated, only the composite is. To prove matter's reality, Aquinas walks through the parallel with other kinds of change: motion in place, growth, and alteration all require some persisting subject beneath contrary states, and substantial change (coming-to-be and passing-away) requires the same kind of underlying subject, which is precisely what matter is. He cautions, though, that having matter for one kind of change does not entail having matter for every kind.",
  "B8C2": "Having secured matter as substance-in-potency, Aquinas turns with Aristotle to substance as actuality, that is, form. He presents Democritus's view that all differences among things reduce to three material variations - shape, position, and arrangement - and then follows Aristotle in showing this is far too narrow: things also differ by manner of composition (mixing, binding, gluing, nailing), by position (lintel versus threshold), by time (breakfast versus dinner), by place (the different winds), and by sensible qualities like hardness or density. Aquinas's key move is to explain why this matters philosophically: each of these differentiae, though none of them is substance in the strict sense, plays the role of form relative to some matter, because it is precisely the differentia that completes a thing's definition and therefore accounts for its being. Just as within genuine substances actuality is what gets predicated of matter, so in every other case the differentia is what most resembles actuality. He walks through Aristotle's examples - a threshold is wood or stone lying in a certain position; a house is bricks and timber arranged in a certain way, possibly with a further purpose added; ice is water solidified in a certain manner; harmony is a certain blending of high and low tones. From this Aquinas draws a threefold classification of definitions: those who define by matter alone (stones and timber) describe only the potential thing; those who name only the arrangement or purpose describe the actuality; and those who state both, as Archytas characteristically did, capture the composite substance most completely, since the differentiating formula answers to form and the material formula to matter.",
  "B8C3": "Aquinas treats this chapter as Aristotle's engagement with Plato's account of form, weighing four Platonist claims: that a species-name signifies form alone, not form-in-matter; that form is something beyond the material parts; that forms are ungenerated and incorruptible; and that forms exist apart from sensible things. On the first point, Aquinas notes that whether \"house\" names the composite or the shape alone matters little for the inquiry into sensible substance, since such substance is composite either way - but it matters for whether a thing is identical with its own essence. Where something is form alone, it simply is its quiddity, as soul is identical with what it is to be soul; where something is composite, thing and essence differ, since being a man is not the same as being this man. Turning to the second claim, Aquinas explains that a syllable is not merely its letters plus their juxtaposition, nor a house merely bricks plus arrangement, because the arrangement itself - the formal element - is not built from the material parts; rather the reverse holds, so man is not simply animal-plus-biped stacked together but something beyond the sum. He then answers the Antisthenean objection that a \"what\" cannot be defined, only likened to something else, as silver might be likened to tin: only composites can be defined, since a definitory formula predicates one element, as form, of another, as matter, while the ultimate simple constituents elude definition entirely. Finally he draws the analogy to number: a definition, like a number, divides into indivisible parts, changes species when anything is added or subtracted, and owes its unity not to being an aggregate or a bare point but to form actually determining matter.",
  "B8C4": "Aquinas explains that Aristotle now examines material causation on its own terms. Though all generated things ultimately trace back to the same first matter and elements, each kind of thing nonetheless has a matter proper to itself - phlegm arises from what is sweet or fatty, bile from what is bitter - and several \"matters\" can be involved when one serves as matter for another further along the chain. The same matter can yield different products depending on the moving cause, as wood becomes either a chest or a bed, yet some products demand their own proper matter, since a saw cannot be fashioned from wool regardless of the agent at work; conversely, if one product genuinely can arise from different materials, the operative art or moving principle must be the same throughout. Aquinas stresses Aristotle's methodological point that a full causal account names all four causes, and that these must be proximate, not remote or generic - for man, not simply \"fire and earth\" but the specific matter, moving cause, formal cause (his essence), and final cause (his end, which Aquinas notes may coincide with the formal cause). He extends the inquiry to eternal natural substances, such as the heavens, which may have no matter at all, or only matter ordered to change of place rather than to generation and corruption; and to natural accidents, which strictly lack matter and instead have a substance as their subject. The eclipse illustrates this: the moon is the subject affected, the earth's interposition is the efficient cause, no final cause need be assigned since defects follow from natural necessity, and the definition becomes explanatory only once it incorporates that efficient cause. Sleep is treated similarly, requiring identification of the proper bodily subject and the precise affection involved.",
  "B8C5": "Aquinas addresses the puzzle of things that begin or cease to be present without undergoing generation or corruption in the strict sense - points, and forms generally. It is not whiteness that comes to be, he explains, but the wood that comes to be white, since whatever properly comes to be does so from some underlying subject and becomes some determinate thing; bare forms, lacking such a subject of their own, are not properly generated but only said to arise incidentally. It follows that not everything has matter, but only what is capable of per se change into something else; what merely is or is not, without such transition, has no matter in the strict sense. Aquinas then works through the puzzle of how matter relates to contrary states: is a body's matter equally potential toward health and disease, is water equally potential toward wine and vinegar? His answer is that matter stands to the positive state, and to the form constituting it, directly and by its own nature, and to the privation or corruption of that state only secondarily, through the mediation of the form it first received. Thus water is properly the matter of wine, and becomes \"matter\" for vinegar only by way of wine's corruption; wine itself is not called potential vinegar, nor is a living man called potentially a corpse, because these are accidental corruptions - vinegar succeeds wine, and a corpse succeeds a living body, the way night succeeds day, rather than the way an effect arises from its matter. Aquinas concludes that anything undergoing this kind of transformation must first be resolved back into a more basic common matter before the new thing can emerge from it again.",
  "B8C6": "Aquinas presents this chapter as Aristotle's resolution of an earlier puzzle: what makes a definition, or a number, genuinely one thing rather than a mere heap of parts - just as bodies achieve unity through contact or adhesion, a definition is one not by verbal stringing-together, as the Iliad is one poem only by convention, but by referring to a single reality. The sharpest form of the puzzle concerns man: why is he one thing rather than two, animal plus biped, especially on the Platonist supposition that Animal-itself and Biped-itself are separately subsisting Forms in which individual men merely participate - then a man would seem composed of two distinct participations rather than being truly single. Aquinas explains that so long as one treats the elements of a definition as detached universals needing to be glued together from outside, the puzzle cannot be solved. But once matter and form are recognized as related as potency to act, the difficulty dissolves, since asking what unites them is like asking what unites \"round\" and \"bronze\" in a bronze sphere: nothing further is needed beyond whatever causes the potential sphere to become actual, namely the efficient agent in things generated. Matter, he notes, can be sensible or purely intelligible, as in mathematics, where a circle is a plane figure, the surface functioning as matter and roundness as form; every definition contains an element of matter alongside one of actuality. But whatever has no matter at all is of itself, immediately, a unity, needing no further cause of its oneness, since being and unity are not separate genera standing over it. He closes by dismissing rival explanations, such as \"participation\" or calling the soul's union with the body a mere \"communion,\" since these only restate the puzzle: proximate matter and form are, in truth, one and the same reality regarded first potentially, then actually.",
  "B9C1": "Having finished treating being as it is divided among the ten categories, Aristotle turns to a further division of being that cuts across all of them: being as potency and being as actuality. Aquinas opens by sorting out the many things people mean by 'can' or 'power.' Some are called potencies only by a loose analogy, as when geometers speak of one magnitude being the 'power' of another; these he sets aside as not truly relevant. What remains are potencies properly so called, and all of these, he insists, trace back to one primary sense: an active potency, which is a source or principle of change in something else, or in the same thing insofar as it is other than itself. From this primary notion of active power, Aquinas shows how the correlative notion of passive potency follows — the capacity in a thing to be acted upon and altered by an outside source. He is careful to note that a thing does not, properly speaking, act upon itself as one and the same subject, since acting and being acted upon require some real distinction between mover and moved. Finally Aquinas explains impotency, or incapacity, as simply the privation corresponding to potency: wherever a power could belong to a subject by nature, its absence counts as a privation only if the subject is of a kind that should have had it. This opening lectio thus lays the groundwork for the entire book by establishing act and potency as principles that run through every category of being, prior even to the distinction of substance from accident.",
  "B9C2": "Aquinas next distinguishes potencies according to the kind of subject in which they reside: some belong to things without soul, some to living things, and among these, some are guided by reason and some are not. This division lets Aristotle explain a striking asymmetry. A non-rational power, like the capacity of fire to heat, produces only a single, fixed effect — fire cannot also cool. But a rational power, such as the medical art, is capable of opposite effects: the same science that can restore health can also, if misapplied, induce disease. Aquinas explains why: a rational account, or 'formula,' in the mind grasps a thing and its privation by the very same act of understanding, though not in the same way — the science is directed essentially toward the positive term (health) and only accidentally, by way of contrariety, toward its absence (sickness). Because the soul possessing this rational principle can originate motion toward either of two contrary termini from one and the same source of knowledge, the rational art is naturally suited to opposite effects, unlike a merely natural power tied to one determinate outcome. Aquinas underscores that this is not a defect in reason but rather evidence of reason's higher, more universal mode of operating, since it possesses the formula of both a thing and its lack. He closes by noting that the power to do a thing well presupposes the power merely to do it, though not conversely — one can do a thing without doing it well, but not do it well without being able to do it at all.",
  "B9C3": "Here Aquinas takes up Aristotle's confrontation with the Megarian school, who held that a thing has a power only while it is actually exercising it — so that a builder who is not currently building would not really be capable of building at all. Aquinas lays out the absurdities this view generates. First, no one could be called an artisan except while working, so that a builder loses and instantly reacquires his skill every time he stops and starts. Second, sensible qualities like heat or sweetness would exist only while actually being perceived, collapsing into the relativism associated with Protagoras. Third, a sighted person not currently seeing would have to be called blind, and blind again and again throughout the day. Fourth, and most seriously, the Megarian position destroys the very possibility of motion and coming-to-be, since whatever is not presently in a given state could never truly be said capable of reaching it. Having demolished this position, Aquinas explains Aristotle's constructive alternative: potency and actuality are genuinely distinct, and a thing can rightly be called potential — capable of being or of some activity — provided that positing it as actually so involves no impossibility. This criterion, that nothing impossible follows from supposing the potency actualized, becomes the working definition of potency henceforth in the book, and Aquinas stresses that it is compatible with a thing's capacity never in fact being realized, since potency does not entail future actuality, only the absence of intrinsic contradiction.",
  "B9C4": "Aquinas continues unpacking the criterion just established by clarifying an important distinction between what is merely false and what is strictly impossible. Something can be false yet still possible — it is false that a certain man is standing, but nothing prevents his standing. By contrast, some suppositions are not merely false but impossible outright, because affirming them as actual entails a contradiction; Aquinas takes Aristotle's example of the diagonal of a square being commensurable with its side, which cannot even be supposed actual without absurdity, unlike the mere falsehood that it is being measured right now. Aquinas then walks through the more technical, syllogism-like argument Aristotle constructs about possibility: if the being of A necessarily entails the being of B, then the mere possibility of A must likewise entail the possibility of B, since if B could not possibly be, positing A as actual would generate an impossibility, which would show A itself was never truly possible. Aquinas treats this as establishing a general logical law governing how possibility distributes across valid entailments, reinforcing that potency is not an arbitrary or loose way of speaking but tracks strict relations of non-contradiction. This careful logical excursus, in Aquinas's reading, is not a digression but Aristotle's way of making the earlier definition of potency rigorous, so that the whole subsequent treatment of act and potency rests on a criterion that can withstand dialectical challenge.",
  "B9C5": "Aquinas notes Aristotle's threefold division of the origins of potencies: some are simply innate, like the senses; some come through practice or repeated exercise, like the flute-player's skill; and some are acquired by instruction, like the arts. Potencies of the second and third sort require the agent first to perform the relevant acts before the stable power is possessed, whereas natural, passive potencies like sensation need no such prior exercise. Aquinas then addresses a question central to the book: given that a rational potency is indifferently open to contrary effects, what determines which effect actually follows when the potency is exercised? Since a rational power such as an art or science could equally produce either of two opposite results, something beyond the bare power itself must settle the matter, or else contrary effects would follow simultaneously, which is impossible. Aquinas explains that this further determining factor is appetite or will: whichever of the two outcomes the agent decisively desires, under the right conditions — namely, when the patient is suitably disposed and no external hindrance intervenes — is what actually comes to be. Non-rational potencies, lacking any such internal principle of determination, act of necessity whenever agent and patient are duly conjoined, and so are restricted to one effect only. This lectio thus supplies part of the deep structure behind Aquinas's later, fuller treatment of free will and voluntary action: rational agency requires an additional principle of self-determination that mere natural causality does not.",
  "B9C6": "Turning at last from potency to actuality itself, Aquinas observes that Aristotle refuses to give actuality a strict definition, since it is among the most basic notions and definitions must proceed from what is prior and better known. Instead Aristotle proceeds by induction and proportional example, and Aquinas walks through each: as the statue of Hermes is to the block of wood from which it might be carved, as the half-line is to the undivided whole line, as one who is awake is to one asleep, as one who is actually seeing is to one who merely has the power of sight — in each pair, the second term is potential and the first actual. Aquinas notes Aristotle's caution that actuality is said only by analogy across such varied cases, not univocally, since some pairs relate as form to matter and others as an operation to its capacity. He then addresses two special cases, the infinite and the void, whose potential existence differs from ordinary cases: the infinite never comes to be actual as a completed whole but exists potentially only in the sense that its process of division can always continue, its potency serving thought rather than terminating in an actual separate existence. Finally, and most importantly for the book's larger argument, Aquinas explains Aristotle's distinction between incomplete motions (kinesis) — such as walking, building, or being healed, none of which possess their end within themselves at any given instant — and complete actualities (energeia), such as seeing, understanding, or living well, in which the activity and its perfection coincide at every moment it occurs. This distinction between imperfect process and perfect act becomes central to Aquinas's later account of contemplative happiness.",
  "B9C7": "Aquinas here takes up the question of precisely when something should be called potentially another thing, since not every subject is potentially just anything whatsoever. Earth is not yet potentially a man, nor even potentially a statue, until it has undergone the intervening transmutations needed to prepare it. Aquinas distinguishes proximate from remote potency: a thing is potentially something in the full sense only when nothing further needs to be added, removed, or changed for it to become that thing through its own agency or through an external agent already at hand, without which nothing impossible would follow. Seed, for instance, is not yet potentially a man in this proximate sense, since it must first be deposited and undergo considerable change; only once it has developed appropriately does it become proximately potential. Aquinas then draws out Aristotle's linguistic observation about denominative or 'adjectival' predication: we do not call a casket 'earth' but rather call the wood it is made from 'wooden,' and its ultimate matter is named by an adjectival, indeterminate form of speech rather than a straightforward substantial name — signifying that this underlying matter stands to the finished thing precisely as potency to act. Pursuing this chain of matter behind matter, Aquinas explains, brings us finally to prime matter, which is no longer denominated from anything further behind it, since it is the ultimate subject, in itself utterly indeterminate, bearing no formal determination of its own apart from the forms and accidents received into it.",
  "B9C8": "This long chapter draws the book's central conclusion, and Aquinas devotes several lectiones to unpacking its threefold demonstration that actuality is unqualifiedly prior to potency. First, actuality is prior in intelligibility or definition, since potency can only be defined by reference to the corresponding act — to be capable of building is defined as being able to produce the act of building, so the concept of the act must already be grasped first. Second, actuality is prior in time, in a qualified sense: though this individual potential man precedes his own actuality, some other thing already actual (a father, a prior craftsman) must always precede and produce whatever exists potentially, since nothing brings itself from potency into act without some pre-existing actual cause; Aquinas notes here Aristotle's resolution of the sophistic puzzle about how a learner can begin practicing an art he does not yet possess, explaining that in any process of becoming, some portion of the end is already partially actual. Third, and most fundamentally, actuality is prior in perfection and substantial being, since whatever comes later in a process of generation is more complete in form, and potency exists entirely for the sake of the act toward which it tends, as sight exists for the sake of seeing rather than seeing for the sake of possessing sight. Within this, Aquinas develops the distinction between operations that terminate within the agent itself, such as seeing, understanding, and willing — the very operations in which Aquinas locates beatitude — and those that pass into external matter, like building. Finally, Aquinas follows Aristotle in showing that eternal, incorruptible things are pure actuality without any admixture of potency, since every potency is inherently a potency for opposites, including non-being, so that anything genuinely eternal can involve no real potency to not-exist; from this he also derives Aristotle's rejection of separately subsisting Platonic Forms, which would absurdly have to be inferior in actuality to the very sciences and motions that merely participate them.",
  "B9C9": "Aquinas explains Aristotle's argument that actuality surpasses potency not only in being but in value. A mere capacity is, by its nature, indifferently open to opposite outcomes — the same power that can produce health can equally produce sickness, the same capacity that can build can also destroy. But contrary actualities can never coexist in the same subject at once, and since among any pair of contraries the good one must be superior, while bare potency as such is neutral between them, the actuality that determinately realizes the good must be better than the undetermined capacity for it. Aquinas notes the parallel but reversed case for evil: since evil, too, only befalls a subject already having potency toward it, actual evil is worse than potential evil, yet evil itself has no independent nature or substance of its own — it is always something posterior, a privation attaching to potency, and for this reason no eternal, purely actual being can admit any evil, defect, or corruption whatsoever. Aquinas then turns to Aristotle's illustration from geometry: theorems that could in principle be discovered simply by dividing a given figure are not thereby already known, but come to be known only once the geometer actually performs the construction — drawing the auxiliary line, for instance, that makes a theorem about the triangle's angles evident at a glance. Aquinas reads this as confirming that potency is brought to light and actualized only through some actually existing operation, here the geometer's own actual reasoning, reinforcing throughout the chapter that actuality, not potency, is the more excellent and more truly explanatory principle.",
  "B9C10": "In the closing lectio, Aquinas explains a further sense of being and non-being beyond the categories and beyond act and potency: being and non-being as true and false. Truth and falsity, he explains, follow upon the intellect's act of combining or dividing concepts in judgment, and are measured by whether that combination or division corresponds to how things actually stand in reality — thinking separate what is really separate, or united what is really united, yields truth, and thinking the reverse yields error; crucially, it is the state of the thing that makes the judgment true, not the judgment that makes the thing so. Regarding necessary or eternal states of affairs, Aquinas notes that the corresponding judgment, once true, cannot become false, nor once false become true, whereas concerning contingent matters the same statement may be true at one time and false at another. He then turns to Aristotle's more difficult case of simple, non-composite realities — essences grasped by a single act of understanding rather than by combining subject and predicate. Here, Aquinas explains, falsity in the strict sense cannot occur, since there is no composition to misjudge; the intellect either attains such an essence, which counts as truth or 'contact,' or else fails to attain it at all, which is simply ignorance rather than positive error, unlike the more complete privation involved in blindness. Aquinas concludes that such simple, wholly actual substances, being incapable of any composition of potency with act in their being, cannot come to be or cease to be, and consequently the intellect's relation to them admits no fluctuation between truth and falsity across time, only knowledge or its absence."
};
